{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues","id":1036789,"title":"Wisdom Issues","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-23T13:20:14-08:00","id":1036789,"title":"Wisdom Issues","content":"As much as I love the idea of Wisdom, IMO it doesn't really work because it can't decide what it wants to be.<br /> <br /> If it's about control over your magic and not a morality system, then premeditated murder shouldn't even be a Wisdom Sin.<br /> <br /> If it's a morality system, then premeditated murder should be worse than manslaughter.<br /> <br /> Inuring doesn't make sense either. Why does the Abyss even &quot;care&quot; about your Sins. The Abyss isn't &quot;evil&quot; it's just &quot;untrue&quot;.<br /> <br /> It's such a melange of contradictory ideas that it's almost impossible to explain to new players in it's current form. That's why it comes up so much around here.<br /> <br /> IMO it's broken right now and needs fixing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1470,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","user":"Johnny Awesome","date":". 01-23-2017, 04:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T13:58:38-08:00","id":1036817,"title":"","content":"Wisdom isn't about, necessarily, being a good person, it's about being a responsible one. Wisdom is knowing when <i>not</i> to use magic, or using magic in a way that impacts yours or others' sense of self, even if you have the option to do so. It's why premeditated murder is considered less dubious than a crime of passion, because at least if it's premeditated you've thought through the potential options and consequences.<br /> <br /> Mages live in symbolic reality, and if they invest in their magical identity, they start to <i>become</i> symbols. Game mechanics encourage this. You have Praxes which become integral to your character's style. They get an exceptional success, and you get back Willpower and Mana. You decode the symbols of one of your Obsessions, great! You gain Mana and Arcane Beats to incorporate into your mystical self, making the magic, the symbol, a part of you. Wisdom is also a magical symbol, a symbol of your control over magic and sense of self (the Mad are what happens when you bottom out on Wisdom. They are basically just conduits of Supernal power. Magic sort of happens around them, especially if they don't &quot;let it out&quot; by pursuing their own Obsessions) and as such regaining Wisdom must, by the game rules, become an Obsession.<br /> <br /> When Wisdom falls, certain things don't bother you anymore. An average PC (Wisdom 7-4) suffers breaking points when:<br /> <br /> - They cause someone else a Breaking Point, damaging the Integrity of their soul<br /> - Doing something that is considered self-mutilating (making a soul stone, amongst other things)<br /> - Not containing Paradox, which violates reality in general<br /> - Forcing a sapient being (Sleeper, spirit, or otherwise) to change its nature long term or act counter to its interests, or binding it to a task. This has some leeway if the action you're forcing them to take is <i>for their benefit</i> or isn't counter to their interests.<br /> - Destroying an Awakened Soul (sleeper souls are more or less fine)<br /> - Destroying a Supernal Being via exposure to the Fallen World (which ends with it falling to the Abyss)<br /> - Further the Abyss and dealing with its agents<br /> <br /> Besides the point, whenever you suffer a Breaking Point, even if it fails, you gain an Arcane Beat. If you fail, you get a Condition, which really means you get <i>two</i> Arcane Beats out of it, and the Conditions encourage you to use <i>more</i> magic, gaining more Arcane Beats. Pushing at the limits of your Wisdom can give you power, because it's just another Mystery. You can also choose to Inure one spell per dot of Gnosis, which means even if it would normally cause a Breaking Point it doesn't, but as a consequence, the spell always causes Paradox. Inuring a spell means telling reality that you aren't bothered by this action, even though you normally would be, and this is counter to how reality normally works. You basically become a walking deviation in reality, an unfolding expression of symbolism that can act with agency and causes existence to fray around the edges. You are a symbol that defines itself.<br /> <br /> <b>TLDR:</b> Wisdom 2E is a symbol of your self-knowledge, and is all about knowing when <i>not</i> to use magic. When you do something that damages reality, even if it only damages the 'reality' of yourself or another person, the symbol that is Wisdom starts to break. If you have the Gnosis (Insight) you can tell reality that certain things don't affect you. It keeps, but it is a deviation from how things are, and thus that action always incurs Paradox. The Abyss <i>doesn't </i>care about your sins. It cares that your actions start to crack reality, even if it is only the reality of another person.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 01-23-2017, 05:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T14:32:05-08:00","id":1036841,"title":"","content":"I agree with you, Johnny. Wisdom is a really inelegant system that tries to have its cake and eat it too. Sadly, I've yet to discover something decent to replace it.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a> What reason is there ever to <i>not</i> use magic? I mean, yeah, there are consequences, but why should a person ever actually care about those consequences? Why are some consequences &quot;good&quot; (i.e., they don't incur a Wisdom break) and some &quot;bad&quot;? Likewise, aren't you <i>more</i> in control taking actions you actively want rather than actions you actively don't? It seems paradoxical (ha) that a Mage should retain Wisdom for feeling guilt over an action they ostensibly wish they didn't do (belying lack of ability to control themselves) than feeling comfortable with the choices they made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T15:38:16-08:00","id":1036872,"title":"","content":"I look at Wisdom as a way to gauge the health of your relationship with the Supernal which has a feedback effect on the health of your soul. Inuring allows a Mage to force his will onto the Supernal without further damaging his soul as a result, but the Supernal lashes back by not allowing the Mage full control of the offending magic (Paradox risk). The relationship between a Mage and the Supernal is also personal, the listed AoH are just examples. The Supernal sets out its rules to a Mage via the Path the Mage is on, and the Mage sets out his expectations to the Supernal via the symbols he interacts with and synthesizes into himself on his path to Ascension.<br /> <br /> To me a Mastigos employing mind control shouldn't suffer an AoH, but an Obrimos with Mind does unless Mind is a primary Arcana via a legacy especially if the legacy is built on the idea of dominance.<br /> <br /> There are some things that could be universal AoH, slaying a Supernal entity for instance. I think I'd put murder in there as one of those customizable things, and have the chances of it being triggered by Order. The FC for instance might risk it with any murder unless their victim is a &quot;Follower of the Lie.&quot;<br /> <br /> This has some interplay with Inferior Arcana as well, a Mage performing an action with an IA might be more likely to suffer an AoH than if he'd used another Arcana for a similar result.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 01-23-2017, 06:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T15:55:53-08:00","id":1036882,"title":"","content":"It's important to remember that Acts of Hubris =/= Wisdom. Wisdom represents a Mage's ability to control her magic, and accordingly, lower Wisdom makes her long-term Nimbus reach further and Paradox harder for her to contain. Acts of Hubris are acts that violate a moral code (albeit a moral code with a tinge of blue and orange). Committing them carries a risk of losing control over one's magic. Accordingly, Acts of Hubris trigger Wisdom degeneration. This does imply that there is a direct causal link between adherence to this moral code and ability to control one's Magic. This does not contradict the statement that Wisdom represents a Mage's ability to control her magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T17:23:23-08:00","id":1036930,"title":"","content":"Wisdom is representative of your Will, in that you define how much you define/allow the consequences of your action to be a result of your intent. It's a loose metaphysical construct of your mastery over yourself-as-magical-symbol as determined by the control you exert over all things that are yourself(you are your consequences, as a nutshell). It's vaguely ethical, though not moral.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T17:43:46-08:00","id":1036939,"title":"","content":"I'm glad I'm not the only one with the inability to understand the very different ways it's sold <br /> <br /> Either way, just anure yourself to a spell and murder people with it and you're fine","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1377-wrequis","name":"Wrequis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T18:48:22-08:00","id":1036980,"title":"","content":"That's kind of the point. I don't think the devs are stupid enough to paint themselves into a corner of what's right and wrong or wise and hubris. CofD is by generally a tool box, and Mage in particular doesn't go around defining hard edges to its cosmology that can't be challenged by a ST or player.<br /> <br /> On the one hand it seems to exemplify Platonic ideals, on the other it seems to embrace a more Pragmatic approach. It can make it difficult to nail some shit down, but I think I prefer that kind of philosophical quandary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T20:02:24-08:00","id":1037020,"title":"","content":"I don't think the ambiguity is clever. The very fact that there are 3 or 4 different takes on Wisdom in this thread I think proves my point that it's horribly designed right now. I realize that people muddle through and figure something out for their game, but it's a shame that it's so contradictory unless you fix it at your table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1470,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","user":"Johnny Awesome","date":". 01-23-2017, 11:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T22:42:22-08:00","id":1037055,"title":"","content":"It's entirely within the rules as written to remove murder as an Act of Hubris, FYI. If you're actually talking about design, rather than explanation.<br /> <br /> You said it needs fixing, right? You gave a simple conditional in your second sentence. RAW, remove murder from the list of Acts of Hubris.<br /> <br /> Save yourself the metaphysical explanation of how the Oneiros interacts with Integrity and the Awakened Soul and Paradox and the Abyss.<br /> <br /> Just do the thing you said needed doing and be done with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T23:40:00-08:00","id":1037062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037020#post1037020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think the ambiguity is clever. The very fact that there are 3 or 4 different takes on Wisdom in this thread I think proves my point that it's horribly designed right now. I realize that people muddle through and figure something out for their game, but it's a shame that it's so contradictory unless you fix it at your table.</div> </div> </div> </div>So how would you actually fix it? At the moment you're essentially just telling people who don't have a problem that they're wrong. That's not terribly productive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T23:44:06-08:00","id":1037064,"title":"","content":"In my interpretation, Wisdom is Wall you put before Supernal Magic controls <i>you</i>, <b>instead</b> of you <u>controlling it</u>. Just look on what happens when you hit Wisdom 0 - you become Mad ( or Qliphoth is you were Scelesti before ). With no Wisdom, Magic is controlling your life, your are govern by the Symbols that you employ as spells. In that view, being Awaken is like being possessed by Cosmic Forces ( Arcana ) that want's you to <i>spread</i> all over the world and rule it every day. By adhering to Wisdom you say 'no, you will answer ME! Not the other way around, you mad Magic!' And in that interpretation, killing someone is only middle problem - every murder is a lot of unforeseen consequences - consequences that will probably need even more Magic to fix them. But still you <i>planned</i> this, so you were prepared and tried to limit repercussions that will lead to more magic needed. And 'killing in rage' is just letting magic totally open, governing you for few precious moment. Kill with rage = Quickly become Mad One = Open yourself totally to be ruled by Magic. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> BTW, we tried to resolve inconsistency with Acts of Hubris list just after premier, to make it more stable for those of us not liking totally open list. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris\">Here is topic about this</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-24-2017, 02:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T00:07:18-08:00","id":1037070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1036930#post1036930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wisdom is representative of your Will, in that you define how much you define/allow the consequences of your action to be a result of your intent. It's a loose metaphysical construct of your mastery over yourself-as-magical-symbol as determined by the control you exert over all things that are yourself(you are your consequences, as a nutshell). It's vaguely ethical, though not moral.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Killing someone in a fit of rage is worse as far as Wisdom is concerned because it wasn't a defined act of intent, there was no considering the consequences, etc.<br /> Killing someone at all is Unwise, doing it without rational intent, or even considering what the consequences might be, is simply moreso.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T02:22:26-08:00","id":1037095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037020#post1037020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think the ambiguity is clever. The very fact that there are 3 or 4 different takes on Wisdom in this thread I think proves my point that it's horribly designed right now. I realize that people muddle through and figure something out for their game, but it's a shame that it's so contradictory unless you fix it at your table.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd argue that as long as the mechanics are clear, which they are, the ambiguity of the definition of Wisdom is a feature not a bug. They're never going to be able to write it in a way that will make everyone happy, so it's better to provide an outline to show you in general how it works and then let you figure it out for yourself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T02:49:08-08:00","id":1037103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037095#post1037095\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd argue that as long as the mechanics are clear, which they are, the ambiguity of the definition of Wisdom is a feature not a bug. <b>They're never going to be able to write it in a way that will make everyone happy, so it's better to provide an outline to show you in general how it works and then let you figure it out for yourself</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. This so much.<br /> <br /> EDIT<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1036930#post1036930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wisdom is representative of your Will, in that you define how much you define/allow the consequences of your action to be a result of your intent. It's a loose metaphysical construct of your mastery over yourself-as-magical-symbol as determined by the control you exert over all things that are yourself(you are your consequences, as a nutshell). It's vaguely ethical, though not moral.</div> </div> </div> </div>And this too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T05:49:20-08:00","id":1037139,"title":"","content":"The examples here contain a huge amount of moral luck, something I'm not a fan of. If what a Wisdom break reflects is a loss of self-control, there shouldn't really be a difference between accidentally beating someone to death with a baseball bat in a blind rage, between beating someone severely but not quite managing to kill them due to circumstances, and even just <i>feeling</i> that same blind rage and being about to act upon it when circumstances intervene. All three represent the Mage experiencing the same things, losing just as much control, but the latter isn't rolled for. Consequentialism doesn't have much place in what is ostensibly a deontological concept (which goes double where the Supernal is involved).<br /> <br /> Likewise, talking about a lack of intent in the aforementioned killing in a blind rage seems odd when we have two Paths whose entire schticks are &quot;You are what you feel/want&quot; and &quot;Your primal instincts are very much a part of you&quot;. If you beat someone to death in a blind fury, you <i>wanted</i> to kill (or at least seriously hurt) them in that moment. Just because your rational brain realizes this is a terrible idea and you'd not have done it doesn't mean that it was very much an action triggered by the piece of meat and salt that is you. Humans like to separate out actions we consider irrational as somehow not being who we are, and that just isn't how reality works. The parts of you (and me) that are dumb, panicky, and that we often wish we didn't have are still very much part of us.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom represents consequences, why are only negative consequences rolled for? Why aren't positive ones? Why is saving a life not rolled for when taking one is? Is spontaneous healing more of a sin than planned healing?<br /> <br /> If Wisdom represents some discord between the Mage's idealized self and the reality, that's fine, but that's a very different system; one that would require significant tracking of what the character's idealized self is and how it changes with time (or whether it does at all). If Wisdom is how good you are at being ethical, that, to me, would be a better system. It also allows more Orange-and-Blue morality as the oaths a Mage swears may become more important to them to maintain the health of their soul than basic common decency. Sadly, such a system is clunky buuut... It might work.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:13:04-08:00","id":1037150,"title":"","content":"Then just use it as an ethical system, it works for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:16:57-08:00","id":1037153,"title":"","content":"It does, and it doesn't; Wisdom-at-present is <i>half</i> of an ethical system, but ethics aren't any more universal. It needs some way to both derive ethics on a per-character basis and then order them.<br /> <br /> I've tried doing this before and it always ends up feeling sort of clunky, but the general idea was to present a small set of ethics questions and then scatter variations on them along the hierarchy of sins. If/when I update it, I might put &quot;breaking a formal oath&quot; on that list somewhere too, regardless of the character's other choices, since that represents a promise before the Supernal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:28:25-08:00","id":1037156,"title":"","content":"I don't see how it doesn't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:39:45-08:00","id":1037159,"title":"","content":"Not to be obtuse, but Wisdom is about being wise. It represents exactly what the name is. Wise with your magic and wise about every day consequences from mundane actions. It can be easily argued that hasty murder is less wise than premeditated execution. It is part and parcel with the comic book axiom &quot;with great power comes great responsibility&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:52:43-08:00","id":1037171,"title":"","content":"&quot;Wise&quot; is a term with a dozen meanings. A Modest Proposal was Swift's response to the &quot;wisdom&quot; of his day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037156#post1037156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see how it doesn't.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just said how it doesn't: It's incomplete. It needs some preferably non-clunky way to customize sins to each character's ethics, since no two characters believe the same thing and there is likely at least <i>some</i> disconnect between the character's beliefs and actions (or else they'd never sin against Wisdom). This sort of thing needs to be hashed out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:53:04-08:00","id":1037172,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037139#post1037139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The examples here contain a huge amount of moral luck, something I'm not a fan of. If what a Wisdom break reflects is a loss of self-control, there shouldn't really be a difference between accidentally beating someone to death with a baseball bat in a blind rage, between beating someone severely but not quite managing to kill them due to circumstances, and even just <i>feeling</i> that same blind rage and being about to act upon it when circumstances intervene. All three represent the Mage experiencing the same things, losing just as much control, but the latter isn't rolled for. Consequentialism doesn't have much place in what is ostensibly a deontological concept (which goes double where the Supernal is involved).<br /> <br /> Likewise, talking about a lack of intent in the aforementioned killing in a blind rage seems odd when we have two Paths whose entire schticks are &quot;You are what you feel/want&quot; and &quot;Your primal instincts are very much a part of you&quot;. If you beat someone to death in a blind fury, you <i>wanted</i> to kill (or at least seriously hurt) them in that moment. Just because your rational brain realizes this is a terrible idea and you'd not have done it doesn't mean that it was very much an action triggered by the piece of meat and salt that is you. Humans like to separate out actions we consider irrational as somehow not being who we are, and that just isn't how reality works. The parts of you (and me) that are dumb, panicky, and that we often wish we didn't have are still very much part of us.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom represents consequences, why are only negative consequences rolled for? Why aren't positive ones? Why is saving a life not rolled for when taking one is? Is spontaneous healing more of a sin than planned healing?<br /> <br /> If Wisdom represents some discord between the Mage's idealized self and the reality, that's fine, but that's a very different system; one that would require significant tracking of what the character's idealized self is and how it changes with time (or whether it does at all). If Wisdom is how good you are at being ethical, that, to me, would be a better system. It also allows more Orange-and-Blue morality as the oaths a Mage swears may become more important to them to maintain the health of their soul than basic common decency. Sadly, such a system is clunky buuut... It might work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Posting my thoughts on this on a paragraph-by-paragraph basis:<br /> <br /> But there is a difference between beating someone out of rage, and feeling that same rage. Beating means that the mage chose to act on that impulse. That doesn't sound like luck to me.<br /> <br /> The Mastigos Path is more than &quot;You are your desires&quot;. The warlock, after Transgressing the boundaries of morals and ethics to Confront her own desires, must then learn to control it. Without that last step, she is a hollow husk whose thoughts &quot;hardly inhabit one's brain&quot;. The psychonaut gains Insight/Gnosis by learning just what desires he possesses, and then learning to wield them as sometimes-literal weapons.<br /> Likewise, the Thyrsus Path is more than &quot;You are an animal running on instincts.&quot; Contrary to its most common stereotype, the Path celebrates the specialness of humanity, and all of its endeavors and civilization, which includes the myriad ethics and laws humans crafted to maintain a working society, which has served well in ensuring the survival of the species.<br /> So yes, mages of all Paths realize that acting without control, without prudence, without Wisdom, is a part of themselves that they must nonetheless strive to tame.<br /> <br /> You are describing actions done in negative and positive intent, not negative consequences and positive consequences. Actions done in negative intent are an Act of Hubris because it means that the mage is both willing to solve problems the quick and dirty way instead of thinking things through, and thinks that she can get away with whatever harm she causes. Actions done in positive intent are usually not an Act of Hubris, because Wisdom is about self-control in the context of &quot;Don't go around harming people with your magic&quot;. Did the mage kill that annoying coworker who pissed him off, who happened to be a serial killer when not at work? Positive consequences, but the action was done out of negative intent, so it's an Act of Hubris. Did the mage save that bleeding victim of a traffic accident, who happened to be a pedophile not detected by the police? Negative consequences, but the action was done out of positive intent, so it's not an AoH.<br /> <br /> I'm still firm on the idea of Wisdom being about self-control in the face of the possiblity of doing harm by your own actions, so I can't quite comment well on Wisdom-as-discord or Wisdom-as-ethics at this point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T06:58:30-08:00","id":1037174,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037171#post1037171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just said how it doesn't: It's incomplete. It needs some preferably non-clunky way to customize sins to each character's ethics, since no two characters believe the same thing and there is likely at least <i>some</i> disconnect between the character's beliefs and actions (or else they'd never sin against Wisdom). This sort of thing needs to be hashed out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Give bonus dices or move Acts of Hubris Tiers based on characters worldview. For example, I let players to get +1 dice to Wisdom roll on self-defense or other 'highly noble' concept, even if they still roll for 'killing in rage' AoH. If they take it to count under their Virtue also, it would be next +1 to roll, so they would like rolling Understanding, not Falling AoH.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-24-2017, 10:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:01:54-08:00","id":1037177,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037174#post1037174\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Give bonus dices or move Acts of Hubris Tiers based on characters worldview. For example, I let players to get +1 dice to Widsome roll on self-defence, even if they roll for 'killing in rage'. If they take it to count under their Virtue also, it would be next +1 to roll, so they would like rolling Understanding, not Falling AoH.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconding this. If all mental gymnastics to justify a given aspect of a game fail, then just hammering it into shape until it does never hurt anyone <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:22:51-08:00","id":1037185,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037171#post1037171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just said how it doesn't: It's incomplete. It needs some preferably non-clunky way to customize sins to each character's ethics, since no two characters believe the same thing and there is likely at least <i>some</i> disconnect between the character's beliefs and actions (or else they'd never sin against Wisdom). This sort of thing needs to be hashed out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, I don't see what's clunky about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:27:58-08:00","id":1037188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037172#post1037172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Posting my thoughts on this on a paragraph-by-paragraph basis:</div> </div> </div> </div>If you're going to quote the whole post, it makes it easier to read if you separate responses into smaller sub-quotes. Not huffy or anything; just a suggestion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037172#post1037172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But there is a difference between beating someone out of rage, and feeling that same rage. Beating means that the mage chose to act on that impulse. That doesn't sound like luck to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is a reason why I said &quot;circumstances intervene&quot;. If you experience the same level of rage but can't act on those desires due to circumstances outside yourself, in what way have you kept yourself any more under control?<br /> <br /> If a Seer by the name of Blood Moon is holding the severed head of your apprentice and tosses it at your feet, your character might beat Blood Moon to death in defiance of the protocol of the meeting. If the character receives the aforementioned head in a box, mailed to their sanctum, they'd be just as likely to want to beat Blood Moon to death but, if any meeting were to come next, they might've cooled off enough to constrain themselves.<br /> <br /> The character, in both cases, experienced just as much fury and anger and rage and loss but in the latter case was powerless to act upon it.<br /> <br /> &quot;Moral luck&quot; is a term meaning when two actors are essentially identical but have different outcomes due to circumstances, and wherein only one of them is blamed for it. For example, if two people meet at a bar, get drunk, and drive drunkenly home, but only one of them has the misfortune to encounter a group of children playing in the dark, swerves to avoid them, and hits a bystander, we only consider that one guilty of a crime (or at least the specific crime of manslaughter) despite the other one ostensibly committing the exact same actions that would render them morally and criminally culpable. That is moral luck; where external circumstances appear to affect the morality of your actions despite you having no control over them.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom is about self-control, the sorcerer experiences no less of a loss of self-control in one circumstance than the other; in both cases, they are overwhelmed by grief. It is simply that there is only one scenario where they can act upon it. Only one would end up making a Wisdom roll despite an identical loss of self-control. Hence, moral luck.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037172#post1037172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Mastigos Path is more than &quot;You are your desires&quot;. The warlock, after Transgressing the boundaries of morals and ethics to Confront her own desires, must then learn to control it. Without that last step, she is a hollow husk whose thoughts &quot;hardly inhabit one's brain&quot;. The psychonaut gains Insight/Gnosis by learning just what desires he possesses, and then learning to wield them as sometimes-literal weapons.<br /> Likewise, the Thyrsus Path is more than &quot;You are an animal running on instincts.&quot; Contrary to its most common stereotype, the Path celebrates the specialness of humanity, and all of its endeavors and civilization, which includes the myriad ethics and laws humans crafted to maintain a working society, which has served well in ensuring the survival of the species.<br /> So yes, mages of all Paths realize that acting without control, without prudence, without Wisdom, is a part of themselves that they must nonetheless strive to tame.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there is a misunderstanding here, and a strong point of contention.<br /> <br /> I was not saying that any Path believes that lack of prudence is a way to live, but more that they challenge the conventional meanings and understandings of it. A Mastigos may believe in a need to control one's desires but they just as frequently see that control only as a way to prevent that desire from inhibiting some greater desire (whatever that may be). In other words, a Mastigos who really loves to get drunk only abstains from descending into alcoholism because they love chasing the Mysteries more, or know the consequences of extensive drinking and are willing to extend some abstinence so that they may enjoy getting drunk more times than otherwise. Likewise, the Thyrsus Path often sees animals as pure in their intents and actions; if they want to kill, they kill; if they want to fuck, they fuck; justifications are often just window dressing for the more honest truths behind it all.<br /> <br /> These things mean that the idea that your desires aren't <i>wrong</i> because the idea of right and wrong don't exactly exist; only the consequences and if they're desirable. Prudence, then, is differentiated from Wisdom. If the aforementioned mentor decides that their grief demands a life for a life, that can and possibly <i>should</i> be justification enough for them. You can say that it won't bring their apprentice back or what have you, but if their grief wants it, why shouldn't they feed it? It's what they <i>want</i>. Sure, you can say that they should focus on healing and such, but why? Is there some objectively better way to be? If they believe that vengeance is a necessity for justice, why do your beliefs overrule theirs?<br /> <br /> I'd also argue that Wisdom and prudence aren't synonyms. After all, Acts of Hubris regularly come up only under negative circumstances. But that's covered in your next point, so we'll come back to that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037172#post1037172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are describing actions done in negative and positive intent, not negative consequences and positive consequences. Actions done in negative intent are an Act of Hubris because it means that the mage is both willing to solve problems the quick and dirty way instead of thinking things through, and thinks that she can get away with whatever harm she causes. Actions done in positive intent are usually not an Act of Hubris, because Wisdom is about self-control in the context of &quot;Don't go around harming people with your magic&quot;. Did the mage kill that annoying coworker who pissed him off, who happened to be a serial killer when not at work? Positive consequences, but the action was done out of negative intent, so it's an Act of Hubris. Did the mage save that bleeding victim of a traffic accident, who happened to be a pedophile not detected by the police? Negative consequences, but the action was done out of positive intent, so it's not an AoH.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would disagree that actions done with positive intent are not Acts of Hubris. For a start, one person's positive is another person's negative. If a Mage hunts down and kills a serial killer with the intent of saving others from the monster's depredations, that's pretty negative from the serial killer's point of view. Likewise, a Seer of the Throne may believe that the Exarchs ruling is the Best of All Possible Worlds but they're the metaphorical and occasionally literal jackboot stomping on a human face forever. A Scelestus may be a nascent Aswadim, believing that the Fallen World is irreparably broken and, thus, the only solution is calling in the Abyss.<br /> <br /> The path to hell, and all that.<br /> <br /> (This is kinda contradicted with Legacies, as well, since Left-Handed ones are all about hurting people. Tremere outright steal and devour souls and never suffer Wisdom loss. That sounds fairly negative to me.)<br /> <br /> Likewise, why is rushing out to help someone dying in a traffic accident <i>not</i> an Act of Hubris? The Mage is reacting on instincts to help someone instead of prudentially thinking it through. Could I make things worse if the magic becomes Obvious? Am I risking the Veil? Why does this person deserve my healing? Are these not all prudential questions that <i>should</i> be asked? Yet the Mage disregards them all and races into the fray, content to play God and prevent events from occurring as they would naturally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037172#post1037172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm still firm on the idea of Wisdom being about self-control in the face of the possiblity of doing harm by your own actions, so I can't quite comment well on Wisdom-as-discord or Wisdom-as-ethics at this point.</div> </div> </div> </div>It feels off to say Wisdom is about self-control in the face of the possibility of doing harm by your own actions, and then talk about positive and negative intent playing a factor in deciding whether something is an AoH.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:28:26-08:00","id":1037189,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> the way I understood it, when Wisdom finally clicked for me, was that there actually isn't a good reason to not use magic (most of the time) and that, for the most part, Wisdom breaks pertaining to Acts of Hubris aren't about what the Mage really feels at all. It's about the other symbols in the equation. The lower your Wisdom, the less you consider the reality of other things in the Fallen World, and the more you see them as just Supernal Symbols. What makes a given thing an Act of Hubris is that it impacts other symbols, and those self-defining disagree rather vehemently with being redefined. The one thing I can see in common with <i>every </i>Act of Hubris is that it either harms reality in general, or harms the internal reality of another self-aware being, whether through causing Integrity Breaking Points, killing them, or forcing them to act counter to their normal behaviour and desires.<br /> <br /> At Enlightened Wisdom, you are extremely sensitive to the reality of other thinking, feeling beings, and what your power over the underlying Patterns of the Fallen World can do. When you use magic to accomplish something that you could easily do with your own natural aptitude or learned skills, then it's Hubristic because you feel the need to rewrite existence (and potentially risk breaking it) to do something you could easily accomplish yourself. If innocent bystanders are at risk, you also risk Enlightened Wisdom. It explicitly mentions <i>bystanders </i>so if you intentionally target someone for a particular spell that doesn't cause Paradox or a Breaking Point, you're pretty much fine.<br /> <br /> At Understanding Wisdom, those things don't bother you anymore. You are a Mage, you understand the Truth, why shouldn't the world bend to your will? Magic flows a little more freely from you, and you don't particularly care about potential collateral damage anymore. Things that impact your self-image, your reality, the reality of others, and the reality of the Fallen World in general, are Hubristic. Damage another's Integrity through Breaking Points? Hubris. Force someone to do things they normally wouldn't or that will actively harm them? Hubris. Inflict long-term damage or committing premeditated murder? Hubris. Don't contain a Paradox and risk the stability of reality? Hubris. Ripping apart your own soul to make soul stones? Hubris.<br /> <br /> At Falling Wisdom, you can't even see other sapient beings as people anymore. You completely deny their reality. &quot;They're just Sleepers/Ghosts/Spirits/Goetia&quot; you think to yourself. They don't have any internal Truth that you should care about, they're just a pile of Patterns and symbols for you to warp to your heart's desire. The only things that affect you anymore are gross crimes to reality. Letting a Supernal Being fall to the Abyss, deal with the Abyss' agents, and destroying an Awakened Soul, which, when you think about it, is sort of the same thing as destroying a potential Supernal Being. At this point, the only thing whose reality actually impacts you is the Fallen World you live in, and once you become one of the Mad, even that is another pile of Patterns and symbols to play with.<br /> <br /> The way I see it, Wisdom is already a pretty Blue and Orange Morality scale, because to my interpretation it's really about symbology and respecting the integrity and reality as other pre-existing, self-defining symbols. Mages see symbols as Truth, the form <i>is </i>the thing, so if you try and force a symbol that self-defines, that declares its own Truth, to become something else, to take on a reality that is detrimental to its existence, then you are damaging an expression of the Supernal. It's also important to note that nothing in the mechanics suggests that a Mage who succeeds a Breaking Point actually feels guilty. They gain an Arcane Beat, representing greater self-knowledge, and they move on. They also gain an Arcane Beat just for risking Wisdom to begin with, so they actually get two Arcane Beats out of the situation. Same with failure, except failure encourages them to embrace their Supernal nature, it encourages them to use as much magic as they can, and to completely disregard the fact that other sapient beings are actually <i>alive</i>. They start to lose the forest for the trees. Megalomaniacal and Rampant also, notably, change the Mage's Anchors, encouraging an even more dramatic shift in their way of thinking.<br /> <br /> <b>TLDR; </b>Acts of Hubris are about denying the reality, and will, of other self-defining symbols and the Fallen World. The lower your Wisdom, the more you see a person as a Pattern, until you can completely disregard the existence of just about everything and everyone, unless you cause, death, maiming, or actively harm reality through the Abyss or destroying pieces of the Supernal World. Acts of Hubris can be enlightening, but failing the roll means you slip into the trap of seeing magic as a solution instead of a tool, or of completely denying the reality of other people. Losing Wisdom means a sudden, unusual shift in the topography of your soul that encourages you to fall down the rabbit hole of seeing individuals as Patterns instead of people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:30:33-08:00","id":1037191,"title":"","content":"I think that for a such an important aspect of setting the chronicles tone one page is not enough. Instead of just telling us that it's just a guide they should write actual toolbox and <i>then</i> say that it's only a guide.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:33:34-08:00","id":1037193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037174#post1037174\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Give bonus dices or move Acts of Hubris Tiers based on characters worldview. For example, I let players to get +1 dice to Wisdome roll on self-defense or other 'highly noble' concept, even if they still roll for 'killing in rage' AoH. If they take it to count under their Virtue also, it would be next +1 to roll, so they would like rolling Understanding, not Falling AoH.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't quite work for me but you do you. Glad you found something that worked.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037177#post1037177\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Seconding this. If all mental gymnastics to justify a given aspect of a game fail, then just hammering it into shape until it does never hurt anyone <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Sometimes, you've gotta just tear it down and start over, though, too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037185#post1037185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sorry, I don't see what's clunky about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's clunky for two reasons. First being that ethical systems are rarely simplistic. It can take a lot of questions to hammer out and keeping track of that for <i>every</i> player in your game can be hell. Works fine for solo games, though. The second is that, quite often, players don't quite know their character at first either. What feels like it works and doesn't can take time to hash out and may change with time. A character may gain a moral stricture they didn't previously believe in during play, or they may find that a previous one doesn't fit.<br /> <br /> I'll have to chew on this, though. The last time I tried to tackle this half of it was before 2e came out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:53:44-08:00","id":1037205,"title":"","content":"Axel, really, let players propose bonuses to their AoH rolls. It works, in that we have base roll on 'sins', but then player trying to defend it by characters perspecitve shows light ON THE PCs PERSPECTIVE itself. Or you as ST is not convinced, just not let them take bonus dice, simple. Players will point you to things she she thinks her character will do, but you also check them so it would not be simply players only perspecitve. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T07:56:20-08:00","id":1037208,"title":"","content":"It's not about bonuses or penalties; it's about <i>when</i> to call the rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T08:00:10-08:00","id":1037210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037171#post1037171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Wise&quot; is a term with a dozen meanings.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/browse/wise\" target=\"_blank\">Wise</a>. Which I say beautifully sums up how the mechanic works and seems to be exactly as designed/intended. You'll note the &quot;different meanings&quot; all fit perfectly, even the slang and archaic, when not used ironically. Speaking of...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037171#post1037171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Modest Proposal was Swift's response to the &quot;wisdom&quot; of his day.</div> </div> </div> </div>The word 'wise' and 'wisdom' are <a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/browse/ironic\" target=\"_blank\">ironic</a> in their usage as you listed them and thus do not convey their actual meaning. In the case of Modest Proposal it is a response to common folly, not wisdom. However that's a lovely reference and I appreciate you including it, even though it is a bit of a bait and switch.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Graham's_Hierarchy_of_Disagreement.svg/707px-Graham's_Hierarchy_of_Disagreement.svg.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 01-24-2017, 11:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T08:19:15-08:00","id":1037230,"title":"","content":"When I start losing my way when it comes to Wisdom and Acts of Hubris, I go directly to the book for clarity. Page 88 defines <b><i>what</i></b> Wisdom is and the trait <b><i>represents</i></b>. Those are two different things. Furthermore, that same section says that it won't even begin to categorize what is <i>&quot;right vs wrong.&quot;</i> On page 89, before they go over the various levels of Wisdom and the Acts of Hubris that risk degeneration, they tell you that these acts are merely examples, this list is not exhaustive, and for you to add or remove anything you want in order to suit your tastes.<br /> <br /> In light of all that, I think criticisms of Wisdom at this point, especially compared to the first edition of the game, are a moot point. If you don't like it, change it. <i>That's literally the Rules as Written.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> -- as for Wisdom being clunky: to your first point, it's not an ethical system and doesn't pretend to be. In fact, it is explicit that it is not. Your criticism <i>assigns</i> ethics to Acts of Hubris. Would you be willing to elaborate on why you think it's an ethical system contrary to what the game states?<br /> <br /> To your second point: That's a good point... that's character growth, maybe, or it's just getting into character. It takes a few spins around the block to break the character in. In those situations I would say use this time to help define the Acts of Hubris and Wisdom levels of your PCs. Put a little meat on the skeleton given the core book. I think that's the reason Mages being at Wisdom 7, the highest point of middling level Wisdom. Wiggle room to figure things out.<br /> <br /> After all is said and done, I think the subtext or theme of Wisdom and Acts of Hubris is for mages to realize that magic is about changing yourself... not the world. You can Awaken. You can do magic. You can become an archmage. You can ascend. You can't do it for anyone else. That's why there are Orders that have sprung up trying to guide people to that stuff... The Silver Ladder, the Free Council, the Guardians of the Veil... And then there are Orders that have sprung up due to the implications of magic... the Arrow, the Seers, the Mysterium.<br /> <br /> You can be Wisdom 1, like Darth Vader, and attempt to change the whole galaxy.<br /> You can be Wisdom 10, like Yoda, and live alone on a swamp world until you ascend.<br /> <br /> The choice is yours and yours alone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T09:04:31-08:00","id":1037245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037208#post1037208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not about bonuses or penalties; it's about <i>when</i> to call the rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Answer is simple - <i>whenever</i> there is shown Act of Hubris from the corebook list. It's <i>players </i>job to find you argument that their character should not go Mad too quickly. THEY should think before acting as PCs, not you. And if you find listed in corebook AoH - we have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris\">created more extensive list of examples</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:07:59-08:00","id":1037269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037210#post1037210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"http://&quot;http://www.dictionary.com/browse/wise&quot;\" target=\"_blank\">Wise</a>. Which I say beautifully sums up how the mechanic works and seems to be exactly as designed/intended. You'll note the &quot;different meanings&quot; all fit perfectly, even the slang and archaic, when not used ironically. Speaking of...</div> </div> </div> </div>The mechanic DOESN'T work that way, though. Every sin listed is something some Mage will do because they feel it is the right action at a given time, and there will be just as many who disagree. Wisdom as a discernment of rightness and truth only works if there is some objective statement of what is right or true. While the latter can be assessed in at least factual terms, the former cannot.<br /> <br /> Hence, the problem from Swift. To wit...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037210#post1037210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The word 'wise' and 'wisdom' are <a href=\"http://&quot;http://www.dictionary.com/browse/ironic&quot;\" target=\"_blank\">ironic</a> in their usage as you listed them and thus do not convey their actual meaning. In the case of Modest Proposal it is a response to common folly, not wisdom. However that's a lovely reference and I appreciate you including it, even though it is a bit of a bait and switch.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you call folly, and Swift called folly, other people called wisdom. To them, starving the Irish was the natural and right way to act; a logical conclusion from their assumptions. Hence, they called it the wisdom of the day. That's really the thing: There is no agreement on what is wise.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar346_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"346\" data-vbnamecard=\"346\">Troy</a> - I fear there may be a misunderstanding here when I call it clunky. May wanna go back a few posts to see the context.<br /> <br /> @Wyrd - That just pushes the question back a step. What is a valid reason for it not to be an AoH? You still need to answer what Wisdom is actually measuring.<br /> <br /> This is all from my phone, otherwise I'd give more detailed responses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:21:24-08:00","id":1037275,"title":"","content":"Like about half of the book, Wisdom is a good idea, but half baked execution.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:24:55-08:00","id":1037278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037269#post1037269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@Wyrd - That just pushes the question back a step. What is a valid reason for it not to be an AoH? You still need to answer what Wisdom is actually measuring.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm Exarchial ST - there aren't any 'valid reason to not be a AoH'. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> There <i>could </i>be one to move one AoH higher or lower in Wisdom Tiers, but you must assume that players need <i>always</i> to roll on those actions, with more or less dices involved. Exarchs made Fallen World this way. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Here are holy guidlines on Wisdom from <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar9102_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"9102\" data-vbnamecard=\"9102\">WHW</a>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris?p=898179#post898179\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Basic guideline:<br /> <br /> - Is the act about something that could be done without Magic safely and without posing risk to the Mage? If yes, it's Enlightened Hubris.<br /> <br /> - Is the act planned and Mage put at least minimum effort into understanding the short and long term consequences of their actions? If yes, bump it up by one category<br /> <br /> - Is the act done on impulse, and/or Mage is giving into their urges? Acting on anger, lust and fear all fall here; if yes, bump it down by one category.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris?p=898192#post898192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More guidelines:<br /> <br /> - Is the act hurtful on physical, mental or spiritual level in potentially long-lasting capacity? If yes, then it's base level is Understanding.<br /> - Is the act leading to permanent destruction on physical, mental or spiritual level? If yes, then it's base level is Falling.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:28:28-08:00","id":1037279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037269#post1037269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The mechanic DOESN'T work that way, though. Every sin listed is something some Mage will do because they feel it is the right action at a given time, and there will be just as many who disagree. Wisdom as a discernment of rightness and truth only works if there is some objective statement of what is right or true. While the latter can be assessed in at least factual terms, the former cannot.</div> </div> </div> </div>And if you don't believe in objective wisdom in reality how could you possibly accept it in a game? Here's the answer to that question: Suspension of disbelief.<br /> Me personally, I believe in objective wisdom and an objective reality even though I'll likely only stumble upon the former by accident and never experience the latter. I have no need to &quot;pretend&quot;, but pretending something is true for purposes of the story is a valid solution. Who sets those absolute Truths (remember this is a game that deals in Absolute Truths, that's what the Supernal is)? The storyteller.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037269#post1037269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you call folly, and Swift called folly, other people called wisdom. To them, starving the Irish was the natural and right way to act; a logical conclusion from their assumptions. Hence, they called it the wisdom of the day. That's really the thing: There is no agreement on what is wise.</div> </div> </div> </div>Calling folly wisdom is as old as recorded history, if not older. It is Gilgamesh and Old Testament old. That any period of folly (this period of time included) calls itself wise fail as an argument that wisdom doesn't exists. It's a non sequitur argument. That like saying being there are liars no truth exists. Or as the Bard said &quot;<b>A rose by any other name would smell as sweet</b>&quot; meaning that names mean less than the observable attributes. People label things sometimes accurately, sometimes inaccurately, that doesn't change the attributes of what is mislabeled.<br /> <br /> Also whether there is agreement on what is wise in immaterial. what is wise or moral isn't chosen by consensus, and if that is your assertion, then your assertion does not fit within a context of a game that has objective eternal Truth being hidden by a Lie as a central theme. Perhaps the themes of the game don't sit well with your ideology.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 01-24-2017, 02:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:41:51-08:00","id":1037288,"title":"","content":"So... what parts of Wisdom even hint at morality other than premeditated murder / dehibilitating violence? Unintended consequences seem to be the order of the day here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T10:53:31-08:00","id":1037292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037288#post1037288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So... what parts of Wisdom even hint at morality other than premeditated murder / dehibilitating violence? Unintended consequences seem to be the order of the day here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. And I'd argue that theme continues with murder. <br /> <br /> It's the one action that has essentially irrevocable consequences in ChroD, even if you manage to 'undo' it - ranging from soullessness to complete de-powerment for the once-dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T16:36:49-08:00","id":1037473,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037193#post1037193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's clunky for two reasons. First being that ethical systems are rarely simplistic. It can take a lot of questions to hammer out and keeping track of that for <i>every</i> player in your game can be hell. Works fine for solo games, though. The second is that, quite often, players don't quite know their character at first either. What feels like it works and doesn't can take time to hash out and may change with time. A character may gain a moral stricture they didn't previously believe in during play, or they may find that a previous one doesn't fit</div> </div> </div> </div>I haven't seen anything in Wisdom that implies that its approach to ethics is simplistic. In fact, I'd say that the open ended nature of determining AoH means they're acknowledging the fact that it isn't.<br /> <br /> I'd hash out an overall ethical code that I want the game to enforce, for instance thoughtless healing can be just as bad as thoughtless harming. Then I'd use the ethical systems of the Orders described in their Hubris section to provide a slightly more individualized system for each of my players. From there I'd just do an ethics checkup when a player buys a dot of Gnosis or Wisdom where I could review the character's history (Preferably with the player) for actions that seemed able to significantly effect their ethical conduct and decide if any changes need to be made to their AoH list as a result. I'd also let the players know that I expect them to be aware of and responsible for their own Wisdom, pointing out potential AoH if I miss them because risking Wisdom is a good way to earn Beats. If they don't, it's not the end of the world and no Beat for them. I think this would make figuring out Wisdom for a player 1) A thing that happens only at certain intervals instead of something you need to track constantly, and 2) Allow the player to develop their Wisdom as they play and get to know the character while also providing a baseline framework for them to interact with. <br /> <br /> Personally, I mostly stick to the idea that Wisdom is about ignoring possible consequences to get what you want. Action matters more than intent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T17:36:23-08:00","id":1037506,"title":"","content":"I feel we're all stuck here by this point. Most of our counter-arguments are being made over fundamental disagreements on certain concepts, rather than logical fallacies the other party is making. Which... is a very healthy debate, actually, but will it go anywhere? We've already had many threads on Wisdom gone down like this in the past.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T17:49:19-08:00","id":1037514,"title":"","content":"If you're not sure if something is an Act of Hubris, ask the player group. Encourage them to ask if they think an act is or not as well.<br /> <br /> Not hard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T18:09:35-08:00","id":1037530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037514#post1037514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you're not sure if something is an Act of Hubris, ask the player group. Encourage them to ask if they think an act is or not as well.<br /> <br /> Not hard.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good advice for playing Integrity and its equivalents in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T18:40:54-08:00","id":1037551,"title":"","content":"I still think it's pretty ambiguous, which some people think is working as intended and clever, but I don't agree.<br /> <br /> Inuring makes no sense to me and might be the key to what is wrong.<br /> <br /> How is it that your decision to make soul stealing &quot;Wise&quot; something that incurs continual retribution from the Abyss? Why does the Abyss even &quot;care&quot;?<br /> <br /> IMO it all started with the Tremere Legacy and players wondering on forums how it could be possible for all Tremere not to be Mad when stealing souls was a big Sin in 1e.<br /> <br /> The solution the devs came up with was Inuring, which of course needed some kind of penalty or it would get abused, which lead to the +2 Paradox penalty.<br /> <br /> None of it makes any sense really.<br /> <br /> If every ST out there has to hack their own version of Wisdom to make it work at their table, then this is a game development epic fail IMO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T18:51:15-08:00","id":1037559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037551#post1037551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I still think it's pretty ambiguous, which some people think is working as intended and clever, but I don't agree.<br /> <br /> Inuring makes no sense to me and might be the key to what is wrong.<br /> <br /> How is it that your decision to make soul stealing &quot;Wise&quot; something that incurs continual retribution from the Abyss? Why does the Abyss even &quot;care&quot;?<br /> <br /> IMO it all started with the Tremere Legacy and players wondering on forums how it could be possible for all Tremere not to be Mad when stealing souls was a big Sin in 1e.<br /> <br /> The solution the devs came up with was Inuring, which of course needed some kind of penalty or it would get abused, which lead to the +2 Paradox penalty.<br /> <br /> None of it makes any sense really.<br /> <br /> If every ST out there has to hack their own version of Wisdom to make it work at their table, then this is a game development epic fail IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that's not true-the first answer was that it was a Legacy and therefore didn't count since that is now what they are, and then the second answer was that becoming Tremere involves template changes with the House as a Legacy, which twice over meant there was never any need to worry about the Tremere Wisdom problem.<br /> <br /> Wisdom has a very clear expectation of what it is and isn't about, but that expectation has an openness as befits a Mystery as well as a tool flexible enough for what it serves in different play groups. That players want a video game instead of a roleplaying game is not the fault of the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T19:22:34-08:00","id":1037574,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037551#post1037551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How is it that your decision to make soul stealing &quot;Wise&quot; something that incurs continual retribution from the Abyss? Why does the Abyss even &quot;care&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>Making it not an Act of Hubris doesn't make it Wise. You don't replace &quot;Using this spell in these fashions causes me to roll dice to see if I lose points in the rating that determines how well I can contain my magic&quot; with &quot;Using this spell reinforces the rating that determines how well I can contain my magic.&quot;<br /> <br /> You've altered your Wisdom to not care about the consequences of a particular spell and as a consequence that spell always invokes the consequences of being careless with a spell.<br /> <br /> Nothing about this has anything to do with whether or not the cosmological manifestation of universal upfuckery gives a particular hoot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T21:40:41-08:00","id":1037656,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037279#post1037279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And if you don't believe in objective wisdom in reality how could you possibly accept it in a game? Here's the answer to that question: Suspension of disbelief.<br /> Me personally, I believe in objective wisdom and an objective reality even though I'll likely only stumble upon the former by accident and never experience the latter. I have no need to &quot;pretend&quot;, but pretending something is true for purposes of the story is a valid solution. Who sets those absolute Truths (remember this is a game that deals in Absolute Truths, that's what the Supernal is)? The storyteller.</div> </div> </div> </div>There, we have to disagree. If you believe in objective morality of any sort, we are miles apart in terms of position we're coming at this from. Which, for the record, is fine; it just means we're going to be faced with a massive disconnect.<br /> <br /> I must call bollocks to that first point, though. I don't believe in Supernal Realms or magic but I can accept it as a feature of the setting. It is the consistency and application and, above all, usefulness of a concept that makes it easy to work with, not whether it's paradoxical or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037279#post1037279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Calling folly wisdom is as old as recorded history, if not older. It is Gilgamesh and Old Testament old. That any period of folly (this period of time included) calls itself wise fail as an argument that wisdom doesn't exists. It's a non sequitur argument. That like saying being there are liars no truth exists. Or as the Bard said &quot;<b>A rose by any other name would smell as sweet</b>&quot; meaning that names mean less than the observable attributes. People label things sometimes accurately, sometimes inaccurately, that doesn't change the attributes of what is mislabeled.<br /> <br /> Also whether there is agreement on what is wise in immaterial. what is wise or moral isn't chosen by consensus, and if that is your assertion, then your assertion does not fit within a context of a game that has objective eternal Truth being hidden by a Lie as a central theme. Perhaps the themes of the game don't sit well with your ideology.</div> </div> </div> </div>See above. If you want a discussion on objective morality, this isn't the thread for it.<br /> <br /> As for Mage, though, maybe there is objective Wisdom, but if it exists, it is wildly inconsistent with any sensible moral code. See: Tremere not risking it for munching on souls. For reasons unknown, being a part of a soul-devouring Legacy that transforms you into a nightmarish vampire wizard doesn't ostensibly impact your Wisdom much, but killing such a monster in a fit of rage would be a sin against Falling Wisdom. If that is a part of the universal moral code, it would appear God crafted it by throwing darts at a board littered with suggestions.<br /> <br /> Likewise, given that different Mages can do the exact same thing to the exact same end result and only one will roll Wisdom, it would seem not to be a great moral judge.<br /> <br /> I don't really like a morality system that amounts to guesswork. I want something that emphasizes core themes and is capable of being extrapolated. Wisdom doesn't need to be a morality meter to be tied to an objective hidden Truth; it can be a measure of, say, how in-tune a Mage is with their Supernal self (as was suggested elsewhere) or simply a measure of the health of a Mage's soul in the most literal sense without any reference to morality. Best of all, these things can be extrapolated upon. Some unknown and unknowable morality meter cannot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037473#post1037473\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't seen anything in Wisdom that implies that its approach to ethics is simplistic. In fact, I'd say that the open ended nature of determining AoH means they're acknowledging the fact that it isn't.<br /> <br /> I'd hash out an overall ethical code that I want the game to enforce, for instance thoughtless healing can be just as bad as thoughtless harming. Then I'd use the ethical systems of the Orders described in their Hubris section to provide a slightly more individualized system for each of my players. From there I'd just do an ethics checkup when a player buys a dot of Gnosis or Wisdom where I could review the character's history (Preferably with the player) for actions that seemed able to significantly effect their ethical conduct and decide if any changes need to be made to their AoH list as a result. I'd also let the players know that I expect them to be aware of and responsible for their own Wisdom, pointing out potential AoH if I miss them because risking Wisdom is a good way to earn Beats. If they don't, it's not the end of the world and no Beat for them. I think this would make figuring out Wisdom for a player 1) A thing that happens only at certain intervals instead of something you need to track constantly, and 2) Allow the player to develop their Wisdom as they play and get to know the character while also providing a baseline framework for them to interact with.<br /> <br /> Personally, I mostly stick to the idea that Wisdom is about ignoring possible consequences to get what you want. Action matters more than intent.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say we came to pretty much the same conclusion; that the only way to make it function effectively is to develop individualized hubris guidelines for each player. I did a bunch of writing on that particular subject earlier today but it still managed to end up feeling clunky.<br /> <br /> Something else that came to me as a conclusion, though, was that it might be best served to follow suggestions from earlier in the thread and toy with the notion of Supernal sympathy. The idea went that, when a Mage marks themselves in the Supernal, they create unbreakable sympathy between their Supernal, idealized self and their Fallen reality via their soul. When the Fallen self acts in ways contrary to the Supernal ideal, it resonates like a note; fraying their soul and weakening it as a channel (with Madness being the straw that breaks the camel's back). This would make explanations easy as to why most commonly immoral acts are sins - the character most likely doesn't believe murder is moral, after all - and yet why it varies between characters. It also explains why certain morally neutral acts, like soulstone creation, are sins too; they damage the Mage's soul. Likewise, any friendly dealings with the Abyss are naturally antithetical to the Supernal self, regardless of intent. Legacies, meanwhile, protect the soul by shaping it to accept the actions involved.<br /> <br /> I also considered changing inuring so that instead of it inuring a particular spell, it inures a particular sin. Thereafter, any action the Mage performs regarding that particular act no longer counts as a sin, but if they cast a spell as a part of it, it incurs a Paradox; a direct reflection of their Fallen self moving out of alignment with their Supernal one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T02:29:35-08:00","id":1037720,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> Inuring isn't about the Abyss caring about you, just because a thing has a risk of Abyssal taint doesn't mean the Abyss is taking an active interest. Risking Paradox doesn't involve the Abyss at all. If a success comes up on the Paradox roll that's the Abyss noticing because you're sloppy.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> I'm not sure we've come to the same conclusions at all. I was just pointing out that the game provides the tools and space for the level of ethical nuance you apparently require.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 01-25-2017, 05:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T08:47:40-08:00","id":1037862,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> I still don't understand why you think Wisdom has anything to do with ethics or morality? Wisdom has to do with what it has to do with and that's it. I think that you're assigning characteristics to it that you don't like and it's causing you to run into a wall with accepting Wisdom mechanics.<br /> <br /> Wisdom has nothing to do with morality but you you say that it does.<br /> Wisdom can be tailored to suit the taste of your games, but you say that is a flaw rather than a feature.<br /> Wisdom, as written, is part of the game and you can't accept it for some reason. If you don't believe in determinism or free will, or if you do believe in both of those things, how can you accept the Fate and Mind arcana as part of the game? I suppose that you do accept those thing, but for some reason, Wisdom is a stumbling block for you. I don't understand.<br /> <br /> As to your thoughts on Morality and guesswork... that's all morality is right? That's why people develop dogma and principles and stuff. That's what the Orders and Legacies are for. It's extremely damaging to may magical control for me, as a human being, to steal and devour the soul of another human being? Okay, then I will change myself so that I am not a human being. I will become a being who thrives on devouring human souls so that I stop losing control of my magic in my pursuit of knowledge and power. No more guesswork. When I'm playing an Adamantine Arrow, I know that it is right to uphold a promise I have made in good faith, even if this costs me dearly. If I take a hit Wisdom for that, so be it. It's physics. It's biology. If I throw a brick or an egg or a ball off the top of a building... it's going to fall. It doesn't matter why I did it or my intent. It matters that I did it. It's gravity. Wisdom is like that. Some people or actions are bricks, some are eggs, some are rubber balls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T09:21:02-08:00","id":1037870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037656#post1037656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't really like a morality system that amounts to guesswork. I want something that emphasizes core themes and is capable of being extrapolated. Wisdom doesn't need to be a morality meter to be tied to an objective hidden Truth; it can be a measure of, say, how in-tune a Mage is with their Supernal self (as was suggested elsewhere) or simply a measure of the health of a Mage's soul in the most literal sense without any reference to morality. Best of all, these things can be extrapolated upon. Some unknown and unknowable morality meter cannot.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>THIS.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037656#post1037656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something else that came to me as a conclusion, though, was that it might be best served to follow suggestions from earlier in the thread and toy with the notion of Supernal sympathy. The idea went that, when a Mage marks themselves in the Supernal, they create unbreakable sympathy between their Supernal, idealized self and their Fallen reality via their soul. When the Fallen self acts in ways contrary to the Supernal ideal, it resonates like a note; fraying their soul and weakening it as a channel (with Madness being the straw that breaks the camel's back). This would make explanations easy as to why most commonly immoral acts are sins - the character most likely doesn't believe murder is moral, after all - and yet why it varies between characters. It also explains why certain morally neutral acts, like soulstone creation, are sins too; they damage the Mage's soul. Likewise, any friendly dealings with the Abyss are naturally antithetical to the Supernal self, regardless of intent. Legacies, meanwhile, protect the soul by shaping it to accept the actions involved.<br /> <br /> I also considered changing inuring so that instead of it inuring a particular spell, it inures a particular sin. Thereafter, any action the Mage performs regarding that particular act no longer counts as a sin, but if they cast a spell as a part of it, it incurs a Paradox; a direct reflection of their Fallen self moving out of alignment with their Supernal one.</div> </div> </div> </div>This idea might be worth pursuing.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T09:28:35-08:00","id":1037873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Troy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037862#post1037862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> I still don't understand why you think Wisdom has anything to do with ethics or morality? Wisdom has to do with what it has to do with and that's it. I think that you're assigning characteristics to it that you don't like and it's causing you to run into a wall with accepting Wisdom mechanics.<br /> <br /> As to your thoughts on Morality and guesswork... that's all morality is right? That's why people develop dogma and principles and stuff. That's what the Orders and Legacies are for. It's extremely damaging to may magical control for me, as a human being, to steal and devour the soul of another human being? Okay, then I will change myself so that I am not a human being. I will become a being who thrives on devouring human souls so that I stop losing control of my magic in my pursuit of knowledge and power. No more guesswork. When I'm playing an Adamantine Arrow, I know that it is right to uphold a promise I have made in good faith, even if this costs me dearly. If I take a hit Wisdom for that, so be it. It's physics. It's biology. If I throw a brick or an egg or a ball off the top of a building... it's going to fall. It doesn't matter why I did it or my intent. It matters that I did it. It's gravity. Wisdom is like that. Some people or actions are bricks, some are eggs, some are rubber balls.</div> </div> </div> </div>But it's inconsistent. It's just as hubristic to heal everyone in the hospital as it is to give them a virus, but only one of those things is a Sin against Wisdom. Why? Because morality IS wrapped up in Wisdom.<br /> <br /> I still can't wrap my head around Inuring. It doesn't make sense to me that you risk more paradox just because you decided to make Fireball not cause you to lose Wisdom.<br /> <br /> I honestly think that the devs loved the mechanics of Wisdom being used to contain paradox and Inuring having a paradox penalty and then wrote Wisdom as it is after the fact.<br /> <br /> The mechanics seem to be driving the fluff instead of the other way around when it comes to Wisdom.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T09:59:17-08:00","id":1037886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037873#post1037873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But it's inconsistent. It's just as hubristic to heal everyone in the hospital as it is to give them a virus, but only one of those things is a Sin against Wisdom. Why?</div> </div> </div> </div>This is factually incorrect, just because an act of &quot;benevolent&quot; behavior didn't show up on the list of EXAMPLES doesn't mean that it can't in fact be an act of Hubris. To claim otherwise is being willfully ignorant of the quotes from the Wisdom section that say, these are examples only, change as necessary, etc.<br /> <br /> I have no idea how you continue to fail to see how Inuring makes sense. You damage your Wisdom (Remember that Wisdom symbolizes your control of your magic), and then instead of deciding to fix the damage the hard way you cauterize your soul so that it &quot;won't happen again.&quot; All the underlying damage is still there, your Wisdom takes a permanent hit to being able to control your magic in that specific instance because you copped out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:08:47-08:00","id":1037920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not sure we've come to the same conclusions at all. I was just pointing out that the game provides the tools and space for the level of ethical nuance you apparently require. </div> </div> </div>Space, maybe. Tools, not so much. That's really my point; I can construct an extra system to make Wisdom into something consistent, but it isn't consistent now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Troy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037862#post1037862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> and <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> I still don't understand why you think Wisdom has anything to do with ethics or morality? Wisdom has to do with what it has to do with and that's it. I think that you're assigning characteristics to it that you don't like and it's causing you to run into a wall with accepting Wisdom mechanics.<br /> <br /> Wisdom has nothing to do with morality but you you say that it does.<br /> Wisdom can be tailored to suit the taste of your games, but you say that is a flaw rather than a feature.<br /> Wisdom, as written, is part of the game and you can't accept it for some reason. If you don't believe in determinism or free will, or if you do believe in both of those things, how can you accept the Fate and Mind arcana as part of the game? I suppose that you do accept those thing, but for some reason, Wisdom is a stumbling block for you. I don't understand.</div> </div> </div> </div>The very first line about Wisdom described it as a Mage's ability to judge when, where, why, and how to use magic. These are all intrinsically moral questions, save perhaps a few engineering ones. After all, there is no reason to use magic or not use it without making a value judgement, and value judgements are for any intent and purpose moral judgements. One cannot even make a statement about efficiency without first declaring efficiency is valuable and setting forth the axiom for why it is so.<br /> <br /> Hence, if it is a moral judgement, it requires a unified moral system on which to base its judgements. One cannot declare something right or wrong without a basis for what is right or wrong.<br /> <br /> Since Mage has largely eschewed any sense of objective morality and neglected deliberately to provide such a code - indeed, there is an explicit statement that Wisdom is at least somewhat subjective - that leaves us with an incomplete system.<br /> <br /> When is it right to use magic? When is it wrong? How do we adjudicate? Is it subjective? If so, why is any act ever unWise? If it's objective, what determines it?<br /> <br /> That is why I can't accept Wisdom at present: It is incomplete. It tells you to make judgements but not how or why to make them and that is ultimately detrimental; it is a gateway to pointless arguments at the table and to watering down a game of grand philosophical questions into bland, conventional answers.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I'm a hard determinist and shun the notion of free will. Not relevant but there you go. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037886#post1037886\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is factually incorrect, just because an act of &amp;quot;benevolent&amp;quot; behavior didn't show up on the list of EXAMPLES doesn't mean that it can't in fact be an act of Hubris. To claim otherwise is being willfully ignorant of the quotes from the Wisdom section that say, these are examples only, change as necessary, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>And yet, Falling Wisdom is described in terms of &quot;Only the darkest, most selfishly destructive acts risk degeneration at this point.&quot;<br /> <br /> So... Yeah.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:12:48-08:00","id":1037921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037886#post1037886\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have no idea how you continue to fail to see how Inuring makes sense. You damage your Wisdom (Remember that Wisdom symbolizes your control of your magic), and then instead of deciding to fix the damage the hard way you cauterize your soul so that it &quot;won't happen again.&quot; All the underlying damage is still there, your Wisdom takes a permanent hit to being able to control your magic in that specific instance because you copped out.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the best explanation I've seen. It helps. Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:14:54-08:00","id":1037923,"title":"","content":"I guess Wisdom should spend more time detailing guidelines for how to classify and construct Acts of Hubris, instead of giving an example list of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:19:29-08:00","id":1037926,"title":"","content":"That would be nice, WHW, yeah.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:34:53-08:00","id":1037935,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037873#post1037873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But it's inconsistent. It's just as hubristic to heal everyone in the hospital as it is to give them a virus, but only one of those things is a Sin against Wisdom. Why? Because morality IS wrapped up in Wisdom.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2632\" data-vbnamecard=\"2632\">Mrmdubois</a> says. Just because it's not listed as an example doesn't mean it's not there. You can look at the capabilities of every arcanum and add them to the examples of Acts of Hubris...<br /> <br /> Death -- all kinds of stuff involving souls or harming folks<br /> Fate -- altering someone's destiny for good or ill<br /> Forces -- harming stuff or even changing forces on a fundamental or symbolic nature (making sounds give off heat or whatever)<br /> Life -- turning men into toads or curing cancer or making your dog as strong as a bear<br /> Matter -- Same as with Forces...<br /> Mind -- erasing memories, creating false memories, subjugating free will or granting sapience to a cat or something...<br /> Prime -- all kinds of stuff... like Death<br /> Space -- all kinds of stuff.<br /> Time -- Almost more trouble than it's worth, right?<br /> Spirit -- has those sorts or provisions covered in the examples.<br /> <br /> You can say in your game that taking a life is the same level of Hubris as saving a life destined to die. All sorts of cool RP can come from this. Like I said, that's what Orders and Legacies are for... Imagine a Legacy of healers that works in a hospital curing cancer and crap like that while Stone Scribes also work in the hospital and believe what the other Legacy is doing wrong, depriving people of their destinies. The other Legacy believes the Scribes are depriving people of their dignity. Philosophical, metaphysical debate ensues. Fun for everyone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:40:36-08:00","id":1037939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037923#post1037923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess Wisdom should spend more time detailing guidelines for how to classify and construct Acts of Hubris, instead of giving an example list of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that would have been a good use of page space.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037245#post1037245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And if you find listed in corebook AoH - we have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris\">created more extensive list of examples</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is useful and seems fairly sound.<br /> <br /> Question about<b></b>:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037656#post1037656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Likewise, given that different Mages can do the exact same thing to the exact same end result and only one will roll Wisdom, it would seem not to be a great moral judge.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given that the two mages have the same Wisdom, and thus it isn't a matter of being below the level of the act of hubris and one hasn't Inured themselves, where are you drawing that two different mages wouldn't be subject to the same standard of Wisdom? (Examples will be examined with &quot;Was it really exactly the same?&quot; as a liptis test.)<br /> <br /> By the way, really enjoying this conversation and hope you are too.<br /> <br /> Here's a hypothetical. Is using Master level Time to rewrite someone's history to prevent their death without premeditation, wouldn't that be an example of benevolent act of Hubris? Also most wonky raising from the dead kind of stuff in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 01-25-2017, 02:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:55:29-08:00","id":1037948,"title":"","content":"It actually says right in the book that two mages don't necessarily suffer the same Wisdom breaking points for the same actions.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom is really about control over your magic, how is it a lack of control to deliberately kill 25 people if you spend 2 weeks planning it? What if you don't use magic to do it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:56:35-08:00","id":1037949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037920#post1037920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hence, if it is a moral judgement, it requires a unified moral system on which to base its judgements. One cannot declare something right or wrong without a basis for what is right or wrong.<br /> <br /> Since Mage has largely eschewed any sense of objective morality and neglected deliberately to provide such a code - indeed, there is an explicit statement that Wisdom is at least somewhat subjective - that leaves us with an incomplete system.<br /> <br /> When is it right to use magic? When is it wrong? How do we adjudicate? Is it subjective? If so, why is any act ever unWise? If it's objective, what determines it?<br /> <br /> That is why I can't accept Wisdom at present: It is incomplete. It tells you to make judgements but not how or why to make them and that is ultimately detrimental; it is a gateway to pointless arguments at the table and to watering down a game of grand philosophical questions into bland, conventional answers.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I'm a hard determinist and shun the notion of free will. Not relevant but there you go.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just here quote sidebar in <b>Silver Ladder</b> book - it's 1E, but I think it's really relevant to 2E Wisdom idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Silver Ladder, p. 39</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Morality versus Wisdom</b><br /> When you read about the Silver Ladder\u2019s beliefs \u2014 Thunder in particular \u2014 you might ask yourself how they stack up to the cold realities of the Mage setting, especially when it comes to Wisdom. At first glance, it looks like any orthodox th\u00e9arch would lose Wisdom at an alarming rate by refusing to moderate magic with humility. Silver Ladder characters can\u2019t get a look at their own sheets but they\u2019d still notice that Paradoxes linger a little longer when they repeatedly throw caution to the wind.<br /> The Ladder does in fact have a very rough idea of Wisdom\u2019s laws and consequences but it\u2019s no evidence they\u2019re wrong. It\u2019s just a symptom of the Fallen World, inseparable from Paradoxes and Quiescence. Thus, magical acts that risk Wisdom loss aren\u2019t necessarily immoral \u2014 but then again, they aren\u2019t automatically moral, either. To put it in concrete game terms, a th\u00e9arch blames Wisdom risks that result entirely from magic on the Lie. Anything that would provoke a Morality check in a human is still a personal failing, whether or not magic had a role in it.</div> </div> </div> </div>In <b>Mage</b>, Morality ( and morality ) is Fallen World construct - there are not 'good' and 'bad' actions - there are only things that are permitted and forbidden by Exarchs. <b>Want to know what actions would be Acts of Hubris? Think on each 'Would Exarchs be pissed off on me for it?'</b> - Chances are high that those with unforeseen consequences or letting magic sling right and left will be the 'worst' from Exarchial point of view. Here is your 'morality' of magic in CoD universe. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Imagine that Exarchs want's everyone mage on planet to be Seer of Throne - if you are not playing by their rules, you break Wisdom, i.e. make Acts of Hubris. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-25-2017, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:03:25-08:00","id":1037956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037948#post1037948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It actually says right in the book that two mages don't necessarily suffer the same Wisdom breaking points for the same actions.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I mentioned before - I have Exarchial bent on Wisdom that <i>works</i>. I throw away this particular line of rules as they really makes everything big mess I complained myself months ago. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037948#post1037948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Wisdom is really about control over your magic, how is it a lack of control to deliberately kill 25 people if you spend 2 weeks planning it? What if you don't use magic to do it?</div> </div> </div> </div>You still kills 25 people and <i>created </i>unforeseen consequences ( you are not omniscience, however you think you are ), even if you try to minimize them. There is <i>no difference</i> between making actions <i>with</i> magic and making them <i>without</i> magic - you <b><i>are</i></b> your magic. All that really matters are consequences of those actions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-25-2017, 03:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:10:00-08:00","id":1037964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037948#post1037948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Wisdom is really about control over your magic, how is it a lack of control to deliberately kill 25 people if you spend 2 weeks planning it? What if you don't use magic to do it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because people who are &quot;in control&quot; don't spend weeks planning to kill 25 people? If that doesn't work try this paraphrase: People who are &quot;in control&quot; don't kill 25 people. Something has gone off the rails somewhere, most likely in their own brain. And 25 people dying is going to cause a lot of chaos and chaos precludes true control. Also assuming you are qualified to dictate the deaths of 25 other people is definitely <a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?q=hubris&amp;ie=utf-8&amp;oe=utf-8\" target=\"_blank\">hubris</a>.<br /> <br /> Also from watching Law and Order all week, that type of atrocity to exert &quot;control and power&quot; over others is actually a reflection of a lack of meaningful control in their own lives.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037956#post1037956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I mentioned before - I have Exarchial bent on Wisdom that <i>works</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what you've said and other people's interpretations, I must also have an Exarchial bent on how Wisdom works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 01-25-2017, 03:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:29:16-08:00","id":1037981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037948#post1037948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If Wisdom is really about control over your magic, how is it a lack of control to deliberately kill 25 people if you spend 2 weeks planning it? What if you don't use magic to do it?</div> </div> </div> </div>You wouldn't risk Wisdom for that. Like, looking over the section, particularly the examples, I don't find anything that suggests that would be an Act of Hubris, magical or otherwise.<br /> <br /> Honestly, you'd have done better with the old &quot;But what about the cop forcing a criminal to act counter to their interests&quot; argument if you wanted to point out fuzziness on the subject.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:31:55-08:00","id":1037982,"title":"","content":"Also, I think it's safe to assume that talking about anyone's particular bent, Exarchial or otherwise*, is not particularly helpful for the conversation. That an &quot;at your table&quot; thing, not a &quot;general design principle at work&quot; thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T13:01:57-08:00","id":1037997,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037964#post1037964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Because people who are &quot;in control&quot; don't spend weeks planning to kill 25 people? If that doesn't work try this paraphrase: People who are &quot;in control&quot; don't kill 25 people. Something has gone off the rails somewhere, most likely in their own brain. And 25 people dying is going to cause a lot of chaos and chaos precludes true control. Also assuming you are qualified to dictate the deaths of 25 other people is definitely <a href=\"http://&quot;https://www.google.com/search?q=hubris&amp;ie=utf-8&amp;oe=utf-8&quot;\" target=\"_blank\">hubris</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>So a military officer who carefully plans a strike on a bunker isn't in control? They're off the rails in their own heads?<br /> <br /> Can you really think of no circumstances in which killing 25 people may not be a just and appropriate moral choice, either? Just because we, in our somewhat safe and comfortable corner of the world, never have to consider such a thing doesn't mean that all possible situations in all possible worlds are the same. Moral judgments like &quot;it's always wrong to kill&quot; are just as subject to disagreement as any other.<br /> <br /> Let's take a hypothetical - <br /> A slave in 19th century America Awakens and plots the deaths of 25 slave owners in a given area.<br /> At what level is this not hubristic?<br /> <br /> One might argue that simply killing them without planning is hubris, because there could be a massive backlash that leads to great harm against the populace (though I would point out that this requires certain axioms to be in place. You have to assume a basic level of morality in order to consider harm negative.)<br /> <br /> What if he extensively planned his act, though? He accounts for the actions of their families, he casts ambiguity over whether or not the deaths were accidental, he times things so that the maximal number of slaves can escape as a result or even personally selects their replacements so that they're kinder masters who he subtly compels to manumit their slaves and adopt different practices, he sticks around to make sure neighbors don't retaliate or make examples of their own slaves.<br /> <br /> A lot of good can come from killing the right kind of person.<br /> <br /> On the flip side, &quot;morally positive&quot; actions (which @Axel rightly points out is a fraught term) could easily result in acts of hubris. And what about inaction? Is it always Wise not to act? Or is it hubris to withhold your greater power for others in fear that you might cause unintended consequences? Believe me, not acting can also lead to unintended consequences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T13:44:21-08:00","id":1038025,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037982#post1037982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I think it's safe to assume that talking about anyone's particular bent, Exarchial or otherwise*, is not particularly helpful for the conversation. That an &quot;at your table&quot; thing, not a &quot;general design principle at work&quot; thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's the point. &quot;At your table&quot; is the &quot;general design principle at work.&quot; That's my contention. And it doesn't take hours of arguing or anything like that. It just takes telling the story you're interested in telling. In one of the quickstart scenarios (Vampire, I think), the characters suffered degeneration if they <i>did not</i> act, rather than if they did. That's a choice and it's not a choice that's going to be made at every table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T13:48:57-08:00","id":1038027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037997#post1037997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of good can come from killing the right kind of person.</div> </div> </div> </div>A lot of good can also come from binding or transforming such a person to a state of being that doesn't consign their return to worldly activity to the domain of time-travel.<br /> <br /> <i>You have <b>magic powers</b> that disincentivize direct harm</i>. If you can't think of a solution that doesn't align you as a magical actor with the corrosive lining of spellcraft, you've already fucked up.<br /> <br /> Magic that lines up with the way the world proceeds normally puts less of a strain on the fabric of reality. Wisdom mitigates the extension of that phenomenon to your existence as a mage and degrades when you make changes that are largely irrevocable.<br /> <br /> Healing a ward full of people doesn't make them stop being hurt, but killing them makes them stop healing. That the former can have significant consequences if done carelessly does not give it the same base weight as the latter.<br /> <br /> Inaction doesn't have much of anything to do with how the trait that measures and is affected by your capacity and concern for <i>how far-reaching the repercussions of <b>using your magic</b> are</i> works, and it's straying into the realm of the Reading Natural Language As Technical Language error to try and apply it in that fashion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Missing word was inverting the meaning of a sentence. 01-25-2017, 04:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T14:54:01-08:00","id":1038050,"title":"","content":"Some people just need killin, huh? That's not hubris at all. &lt;/sarcasm&gt;<br /> <br /> But seriously...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037997#post1037997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So a military officer who carefully plans a strike on a bunker isn't in control?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll refer to what I said earlier. Wisdom doesn't tell anyone how to behave. That's what our Orders and Legacies are for. An Adamantine Arrow or a Guardian of the Veil might <i>easily</i> be able to justify killing 25 people. I think that's just said for the reasons <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar111_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"111\" data-vbnamecard=\"111\">Satchel</a> said in this thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's take a hypothetical -<br /> A slave in 19th century America Awakens and plots the deaths of 25 slave owners in a given area...<br /> <br /> ...What if he extensively planned his act, though? He accounts for the actions of their families, he casts ambiguity over whether or not the deaths were accidental, he times things so that the maximal number of slaves can escape as a result or even personally selects their replacements so that they're kinder masters who he subtly compels to manumit their slaves and adopt different practices, he sticks around to make sure neighbors don't retaliate or make examples of their own slaves. </div> </div> </div>If I can have anything I want in this scenario, they'll I'll have it that the Awakened former slave has the ability to do all these things without killing a single person. How about that then?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You have to assume a basic level of morality in order to consider harm negative.)<br /> <br /> A lot of good can come from killing the right kind of person. </div> </div> </div>Assuming the inverse of both of those statements also indicates some sort of morality. Unless of course this is your <i>nature</i>... You are a tree or a shark or a termite or ebola... and you can do no other than what you are doing. I don't think an Awakened person is confined to those sorts of intellectual or behavioral prisons. Not even a Sleeper is imprisoned so, right?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the flip side, &quot;morally positive&quot; actions (which @Axel rightly points out is a fraught term) could easily result in acts of hubris. And what about inaction? Is it always Wise not to act? Or is it hubris to withhold your greater power for others in fear that you might cause unintended consequences? Believe me, not acting can also lead to unintended consequences. </div> </div> </div>Unintended by whom?<br /> All you have are your choices. All you ever have.<br /> You're going to live by them and die by them.<br /> Make the right ones.<br /> <br /> Everything you say is taken into account when your Wisdom is 8-10... those guys are constantly, maybe neurotically, considering the consequences of their every action. Maybe even they're every thought! And even inaction is taken into account. Imagine what it must be like to be Acanthus and know the cause and consequence of everything you see. Or being a Mastigos and being able to know the intent and instinct of every person, animal, or spirit you encounter. Or being a Thyrsus and fundamentally understanding the nature of everything you see from the man across to the street, to the wind, the birds, and very asphalt itself. Or being Moros and knowing, without a doubt, that nothing you do, nothing you <i>ever</i> do will make any lasting difference; everything changes, everyone is dust.<br /> <br /> That's being Awakened.<br /> And that's what the game deals with. Conversations like the one in this thread are the daily conscious and unconscious struggles of every mage.<br /> <br /> Isn't that cool?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":346,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","user":"Troy","date":".  misplaced quote code 01-25-2017, 05:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T15:51:02-08:00","id":1038084,"title":"","content":"Again - what's inherently more moral about not killing? Saying that there's always a nonviolent option is cute, but nonviolent solutIons require a different sort of follow-up and aren't always suitable for all situations.<br /> <br /> We don't know the capabilities of this mage or their training, we don't know the exact circumstances of what their resources are, what their support is like, what their needs are, what time constraints they're under, so saying &quot;well, they should do this nonviolently!&quot; borders on childish. Terminal violence against an oppressor can be both moral and justified if it is the least harm needed to produce the needed result... but sometimes that still involves violence, and not everyone agrees that killing is the worst thing ever.<br /> <br /> What about the Awakened Wolf-Blooded, who comes from a family of Iron Masters, who has watched her family hunt and kill human beings for the good of the greater organism? To them, death and life are intertwined, and they wouldn't see the value in unnecessarily prolonging a life that is spent harming the public good. They may even believe in reincarnation and that starting life anew is better than attempting to correct bad growth, and that there is no great difference between being living and dead at all. Can that be hubris, forcing your will over another person? Sure, totally, but any action to forcibly alter circumstances for other people involves that on some level. The only meaningful distinction between slamming a drug lord in a Space prison and killing him for the boarder public is essentially non-existent if he doesn't return on an immediate timescale.<br /> Keep in mind that transformation and binding have costs - you need to keep an eye on a bound person, make sure no one ever finds your transformed person. If you lock a Seer up instead of killing him, you run the risk of him getting free and wreaking more havoc than if you'd killed him. You or someone else needs to invest time and energy in protecting him, but is that really a <i>wise</i> use of your resources?<br /> Efficiency has a value in morality when not having to devote resources to a thing means that you can engage in other acts, and killing someone is an extremely efficient method of keeping them from coming back. If you can have magical certainty of their guilt and there's no real way to rehabilitate them especially. Their culling mentality is perfectly moral from their perspective because they have limited resources and so must choose options that produce better results. This needn't mean that they're random, either - they can take just as much care in consequence, managing the aftermath.<br /> That's an alien wisdom, but there it is.<br /> <br /> In the real world, we find absolute certainty hard to come by and so execution is rarely moral, but even in the real world we sometimes find violence morally justifiable and necessarily.<br /> <br /> I personally would not do such things, but a character of mine might, and who's to say it's any less wise?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some people just need killin, huh? That's not hubris at all. </div> </div> </div>Boy, that is so far from what I said.<br /> <br /> Also, Wisdom manifestly is not just for acts committed with magic. Murdering someone with a baseball bat and murdering them with a fireball are no longer distinct on the scale.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":5226,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","user":"Jachra","date":". 01-25-2017, 06:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T16:01:57-08:00","id":1038091,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5226_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5226\" data-vbnamecard=\"5226\">Jachra</a> No one said non-violently, they said non-lethally. Totally different. Although, I see you addressed that alternative.<br /> <br /> \u200bFrankly, I'm more interested in how you'd respond to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar111_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"111\" data-vbnamecard=\"111\">Satchel</a> with his point about how Wisdom seems to be on some level about not doing things you can't undo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 01-25-2017, 07:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T16:07:29-08:00","id":1038096,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5226_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5226\" data-vbnamecard=\"5226\">Jachra</a> - And if you want to make PCs morality views counts - just take out some Acts of Hubris of his or give character bonus dices to roll. Have 'noble cause' or 'acted like this attitude all life'? Give him +1 bonus dice - it changes effects of roll. But character still needs to roll. Because Exarchs made world this way. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T16:33:32-08:00","id":1038103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037949#post1037949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just here quote sidebar in <b>Silver Ladder</b> book - it's 1E, but I think it's really relevant to 2E Wisdom idea.<br /> <br /> In <b>Mage</b>, Morality ( and morality ) is Fallen World construct - there are not 'good' and 'bad' actions - there are only things that are permitted and forbidden by Exarchs. <b>Want to know what actions would be Acts of Hubris? Think on each 'Would Exarchs be pissed off on me for it?'</b> - Chances are high that those with unforeseen consequences or letting magic sling right and left will be the 'worst' from Exarchial point of view. Here is your 'morality' of magic in CoD universe. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Imagine that Exarchs want's everyone mage on planet to be Seer of Throne - if you are not playing by their rules, you break Wisdom, i.e. make Acts of Hubris. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Given that one of the primary artifacts the Exarchs bestow is a thing that lets you magically mind-control people, and that one of the examples of a sin is forcing a sapient being to act against its own interests, I feel the need to call bollocks on this idea from that point alone.<br /> <br /> More directly, in-setting fluff on what the Ladder believes is different from the actual mechanical texts in the core book itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1037981#post1037981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You wouldn't risk Wisdom for that. Like, looking over the section, particularly the examples, I don't find anything that suggests that would be an Act of Hubris, magical or otherwise.<br /> <br /> Honestly, you'd have done better with the old &quot;But what about the cop forcing a criminal to act counter to their interests&quot; argument if you wanted to point out fuzziness on the subject.</div> </div> </div> </div>Premeditated murder is an example sin. What would give you the idea that premeditated murder would not be a Wisdom sin?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T17:15:26-08:00","id":1038114,"title":"","content":"Two types of murder are literally two example sins. I never understood why Wisdom cares so much about killing if there is a whole Watchtower dedicated to the concept that Death Is Not The End and Death Is Not Bad and that Sometimes Dying Or Killing Really Is Optimal And Good. <br /> Again, I would pay for a tool to create Act of Hubris guidelines for characters and or Cabals. Imagine procedure ala Climbing The Ladder, Wisdom Edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T18:27:06-08:00","id":1038153,"title":"","content":"Integrity, Humanity, Harmony &amp; Wisdom, in 2ed none of these are actually about morality or questions of right or wrong, they are about actions and events that move the character in question further away from the person they used to be and they are not always seen as negative, just look at Mummy were Memory moves them away from unquestioning servants towards self determination.<br /> \u200bAxelgear had it right when he said murder is on the list because for that individual it pushes them outside their safe little bubble of who they see themselves to be (or what the developers believe to be human nature). In the God Machine adaptation rules it makes a point that for a hardened police officer the sight of brutal murder has little effect, while a suburban dweller has a breakdown after accidentally running over a squirrel with their car.<br /> \u200bA person with low Integrity/Humanity/Harmony or Wisdom isn't actually insanely dysfunctional, they have just become so removed from what we think of as humanity that they no longer see things the way we do.<br /> \u200bFor a Mage Wisdom loss isn't about evil, it is about changing from being human to honestly seeing yourself as godlike. It is little different from those in positions of power who stop seeing people and only see statistics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T19:06:45-08:00","id":1038177,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038103#post1038103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Premeditated murder is an example sin. What would give you the idea that premeditated murder would not be a Wisdom sin?</div> </div> </div> </div>That is weird. It's in there, but when Dave put forth the idea, Pre-meditated murder was, like, the flagship example of what Wisdom was-pre-meditation as a means of controlling your magical identity/being.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T19:26:39-08:00","id":1038192,"title":"","content":"He did say, in the open development process, 'Mage is one of the few World of Darkness systems where premeditated murder is better than panicked killing in self defense!' In the comments section a few people complained about it, in fact.<br /> <br /> And to be fair, it is a Wisdom 4-7 Act, while 'Killing someone in a fit of rage,' is a Wisdom 1-3 Act of Hubris.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T19:40:28-08:00","id":1038203,"title":"","content":"I'm telling 'yall, Wisdom is a trait that sums up how well a mage can control her magic on a 1-10 scale. Acts of Hubris are violations of an ethical code that, when a Mage commits them, have a chance of causing her to lose a degree of control over her magic (reducing her Wisdom). What this implies about the setting is that a Mage's adherence to said ethical code has a direct impact on her ability to control her magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T19:48:43-08:00","id":1038209,"title":"","content":"That fits with how inuring works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T21:15:53-08:00","id":1038259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038084#post1038084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about the Awakened Wolf-Blooded, who comes from a family of Iron Masters, who has watched her family hunt and kill human beings for the good of the greater organism?</div> </div> </div> </div>Her family are werewolves, who are notably not banner instances of stability despite cultural aspirations to the contrary and do not have much of any truck with human souls outside their peripheral interaction with that species' chunk of the metaphysics.<br /> <br /> Her family is concerned with the health-as-defined-by-the-pack of the world within and around their territory, and one of those worlds has its priorities set by inhuman creatures built on a foundation of spiritual resonance.<br /> <br /> Her family does not have to worry about managing the addictive nature of magical impulse through activity that at least initially bears some resemblance to retaining human autonomy, and in fact may regularly resort to targeted anthropophagy to maintain control over the very different impulses that come from their symbolic heritage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To them, death and life are intertwined, and they wouldn't see the value in unnecessarily prolonging a life that is spent harming the public good. </div> </div> </div>The degree to which conflating &quot;you are capable of murdering this person&quot; and &quot;you are making this person's life longer than it could be by not murdering this person&quot; is absurd cannot be overstated in this discussion about adjudicating whether to roll for the degeneration of a trait that determines how well you do at not spilling your magic all over the place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Can that be hubris, forcing your will over another person? Sure, totally, but any action to forcibly alter circumstances for other people involves that on some level. The only meaningful distinction between slamming a drug lord in a Space prison and killing him for the boarder public is essentially non-existent if he doesn't return on an immediate timescale. </div> </div> </div>Wisdom is not about the broader public. The broader public is not capable of watching a dead man's soul vanish to whatever destination the dying breath flies to or knowing that a perfectly good body just lost its animating force and the knowledge sustained therein. The broader public has no meaningful way of influencing how badly a mage's spells can run amok save through much of the broader public being Sleepers who can't witness magic without getting hurt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Keep in mind that transformation and binding have costs - you need to keep an eye on a bound person, make sure no one ever finds your transformed person. </div> </div> </div>And sadly, there is definitely not an established set of global conspiracies of mages, many of whom maintain safehouses for the express purpose of storing things that you don't want a lot of people to know about or help set up false identities for people.<br /> <br /> And of course there's all kinds of obstacles to turning someone into what is for all intents and purposes a dead body with a spell literally every mage can develop the acumen for without special tutoring and contriving their discovery and burial. Or doing something similar with a coma.<br /> <br /> Lord knows there is no meaningful distinction between &quot;I let a man's soul loose from his body and am aware of and therefore entangled with a symbolic interface which takes a special interest in souls, to say nothing of the people who will be looking for him and will have potentially undesirable reactions if they discover his death&quot; and &quot;I gave a dog to my neighbors and can arrange to have him 'run away' if I should ever need to call in the bargaining chip of having the holder of a particular set of resources at my mercy.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you lock a Seer up instead of killing him, you run the risk of him getting free and wreaking more havoc than if you'd killed him. You or someone else needs to invest time and energy in protecting him, but is that really a <i>wise</i> use of your resources? </div> </div> </div>Technical reading of natural language. If the determining factor for whether spellcraft is an act of hubris is something you'd need to run simulations to figure out, it's markedly too far divorced from the concerns of the game trait as written. The Seers of the Throne do not exude a Wisdom-degrading memetic proximity field just because a character who knows they're around isn't engaging with them.<br /> <br /> <br /> Premeditated murder doesn't utterly break the control you have over your magic, and doesn't even imperil it at all if you use the right spells. That doesn't mean wizards who engage in it regularly aren't going to be markedly different than their nonlethal compatriots in the setting as designed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T21:50:28-08:00","id":1038265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038177#post1038177\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is weird. It's in there, but when Dave put forth the idea, Pre-meditated murder was, like, the flagship example of what Wisdom was-pre-meditation as a means of controlling your magical identity/being.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. Tetrarchs were supposed to be grand examples of high Wisdom Mages who were still evil, for the careful planning they put into their actions kept them at bay from falling into low Wisdom. It'd be nice if that were the case. As it stands, it seems hard to imagine a Tetrarch who wouldn't be in the Falling category.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038203#post1038203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm telling 'yall, Wisdom is a trait that sums up how well a mage can control her magic on a 1-10 scale. Acts of Hubris are violations of an ethical code that, when a Mage commits them, have a chance of causing her to lose a degree of control over her magic (reducing her Wisdom). What this implies about the setting is that a Mage's adherence to said ethical code has a direct impact on her ability to control her magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm very much of this point of view at this point, at least in that it's the only consistent way to explore Wisdom. It just runs into the issue of figuring out what that ethics code is. I ended up trying to conjure up a mechanism but it's clunky as hell so far and needs streamlining.<br /> <br /> I suppose I could use some feedback on it...<br /> <br /> What I worked up thus far was breaking the &quot;standard ethics&quot; down into taboos, universals, and exceptions. Each character picks ten taboos - three mundane, three Path, three Order, and one wild card - and up to five exceptions. Mundane taboos reflect the character's ethics regarding interacting with humans, be they Sleeper or Mage; Path taboos reflect ethics regarding magic in particular; Order taboo, I think you can guess. Wild card can be any or reflect a particular superstition or Legacy.<br /> <br /> Universals are things any sane human being accepts <i>prima facie</i> as immoral or unethical; murder, violent assault, etc. Every character is assumed to have these by default. One additional one was violating a sworn oath.<br /> <br /> Exceptions are circumstances where a given sin is acceptable. For example, a Mage with an Order taboo of burning books (because they're a mystagogue) may be willing to allow it for Abyssally-tainted knowledge. Arrows may consider it acceptable to commit other sins in service of their oaths of duty, and so forth. A hiccup in exceptions is that they're positive declarations as much as taboos are: If you don't act upon an exception, that's a sin. If you don't believe destroying Abyssal knowledge is a sin, while all other knowledge is sacred, it indicates that you clearly think that Abyssal knowledge is worthy of destruction.<br /> <br /> Once it's all set, the three tiers of sins are based on whether the violation is a minor, simple, or egregious violation (Enlightened, Understanding, Falling). A minor violation on the destruction of books would be a mistreatment of a book (throwing it against the wall) or a refusal to prevent the destruction of a source of knowledge; a simple violation is a violation of the taboo in the most direct sense (a Mage burning a book to destroy the knowledge within it), and an egregious violation is a truly blatant and flagrant violation, such as setting fire to a library.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T22:41:46-08:00","id":1038276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038265#post1038265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm very much of this point of view at this point, at least in that it's the only consistent way to explore Wisdom. It just runs into the issue of figuring out what that ethics code is.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's telling that the metric for control over one's magic is called Wisdom and the sins which cause a Mage to lose Wisdom are called Acts of Hubris. From Wikipedia:<br /> <br /> &quot;<b>Hubris</b><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> (</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA_for_English\" target=\"_blank\">/\u02c8hju\u02d0br\u026as/</a></span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, also </span></span></span><b>hybris</b><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, from </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek\" target=\"_blank\">ancient Greek</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%BD%95%CE%B2%CF%81%CE%B9%CF%82\" target=\"_blank\">\u1f55\u03b2\u03c1\u03b9\u03c2</a></span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">) describes a personality quality of extreme or foolish </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pride\" target=\"_blank\">pride</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> or dangerous over-</span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confidence\" target=\"_blank\">confidence</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">.</span></span></span><sup><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubris#cite_note-1\" target=\"_blank\">[1]</a></sup><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> In its ancient Greek context, it typically describes behavior that defies the norms of behavior or challenges the gods, and which in turn brings about the downfall, or </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemesis_(mythology)\" target=\"_blank\">nemesis</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, of the perpetrator of hubris.&quot;</span></span></span><br /> <br /> So, we can gather that this ethical code values humility, caution, and social orthodoxy.<br /> <br /> We can also see from the example breaking points that this ethical code concerns itself particularly, but not exclusively, with the use of magic. The most extreme Acts of Hubris, those at Falling level, concern themselves with the permanent destruction of other entities - human beings, Awakened souls, Supernal entities, etc. Slightly less severe, but still quite serious, are Acts of Hubris at Understanding Wisdom. They seem to largely be concerned with carless or taboo uses of magic - releasing Paradox, performing magic in front of Sleepers, creating soul stones, and subjugating the minds of others. Interestingly, premeditated murder appears here, rather than at Falling Wisdom with the other acts that end lives. This would seem to me to indicate that an Act of Hubris can be reduced in severity if it is performed with intentionality and planning. Above this, we have Acts of Hubris against Enlightened Wisdom, which only mentions using magic to accomplish goals that could safely be accomplished without magic, and harming others by accident. Interestingly, the latter is similar to an Understanding Act of Hubris, causing long-term injury to others.<br /> <br /> So, if you want a mechanism for determining the Wisdom Tier of Acts of Hubris, I would say list a few actions, and have modifiers that can move them up or down a tier. For example:<br /> <br /> <b>Enlightened:</b> Unnecessary use of magic, causing minor or short-term harm to others.<br /> <b>Understanding: </b>Careless use of magic, causing severe or long-term harm to others.<br /> <b>Falling: </b>Self-aggrandizing use of magic, causing death or permanent harm to others.<br /> <br /> If an Act of Hubris involves harm to others, move it down one Tier if it is done with magic, or up one tier if it is carefully planned ahead of time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 01-26-2017, 01:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T23:43:40-08:00","id":1038286,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038276#post1038276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, if you want a mechanism for determining the Wisdom Tier of Acts of Hubris, I would say list a few actions, and have modifiers that can move them up or down a tier. For example:<br /> <br /> <b>Enlightened:</b> Unnecessary use of magic, causing minor or short-term harm to others.<br /> <b>Understanding: </b>Careless use of magic, causing severe or long-term harm to others.<br /> <b>Falling: </b>Self-aggrandizing use of magic, causing death or permanent harm to others.<br /> <br /> If an Act of Hubris involves harm to others, move it down one Tier if it is done with magic, or up one tier if it is carefully planned ahead of time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which are more or less things we made with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar9102_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"9102\" data-vbnamecard=\"9102\">WHW</a> ( and few others guidlines ) months ago <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/897632-2e-fan-guidelines-for-acts-of-hubris\">in this topic</a>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T00:08:34-08:00","id":1038293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038265#post1038265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What I worked up thus far was breaking the &quot;standard ethics&quot; down into taboos, universals, and exceptions. Each character picks ten taboos - three mundane, three Path, three Order, and one wild card - and up to five exceptions. Mundane taboos reflect the character's ethics regarding interacting with humans, be they Sleeper or Mage; Path taboos reflect ethics regarding magic in particular; Order taboo, I think you can guess. Wild card can be any or reflect a particular superstition or Legacy.<br /> <br /> Universals are things any sane human being accepts <i>prima facie</i> as immoral or unethical; murder, violent assault, etc. Every character is assumed to have these by default. One additional one was violating a sworn oath.<br /> <br /> Exceptions are circumstances where a given sin is acceptable. For example, a Mage with an Order taboo of burning books (because they're a mystagogue) may be willing to allow it for Abyssally-tainted knowledge. Arrows may consider it acceptable to commit other sins in service of their oaths of duty, and so forth. A hiccup in exceptions is that they're positive declarations as much as taboos are: If you don't act upon an exception, that's a sin. If you don't believe destroying Abyssal knowledge is a sin, while all other knowledge is sacred, it indicates that you clearly think that Abyssal knowledge is worthy of destruction.<br /> <br /> Once it's all set, the three tiers of sins are based on whether the violation is a minor, simple, or egregious violation (Enlightened, Understanding, Falling). A minor violation on the destruction of books would be a mistreatment of a book (throwing it against the wall) or a refusal to prevent the destruction of a source of knowledge; a simple violation is a violation of the taboo in the most direct sense (a Mage burning a book to destroy the knowledge within it), and an egregious violation is a truly blatant and flagrant violation, such as setting fire to a library.</div> </div> </div> </div>Y'know, I may have disagreeing views with you on what Wisdom is, but this system you've cooked up is In fact, I think this could work as a third &quot;karma meter&quot; trait, next to Morality and Integrity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T06:39:22-08:00","id":1038384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038276#post1038276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-snip-</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I'd say humility and social orthodoxy components here aren't great, humility because it ultimately falls into the latter category. Social orthodoxy isn't something Mages have a great time following; after all, they <i>all</i> defy Sleeper norms, and each Order has radically different dogmas, as might individual cabals, as might Consilia. Whose gets primacy feels like a messy debate that ultimately isn't great. While flagrantly defying social norms might be unWise (at the Enlightened level, if no other), it shouldn't be so because Wisdom is a measure of how much you bow to convention.<br /> <br /> I do like adjusting any and all sins based on whether magic is used and whether it was planned out ahead of time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038293#post1038293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Y'know, I may have disagreeing views with you on what Wisdom is, but this system you've cooked up is In fact, I think this could work as a third &quot;karma meter&quot; trait, next to Morality and Integrity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks. The method itself isn't great, though, at the moment; tracking even the Five Questions of Integrity can be difficult. It needs to be standardized somewhat in a way that slims down the process, standardizes the choices somewhat, or both. Ultimately, an ST shouldn't have a terrible time trying to remember who believes in what.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T06:49:13-08:00","id":1038391,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038384#post1038384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I'd say humility and social orthodoxy components here aren't great, humility because it ultimately falls into the latter category. Social orthodoxy isn't something Mages have a great time following; after all, they <i>all</i> defy Sleeper norms, and each Order has radically different dogmas, as might individual cabals, as might Consilia. Whose gets primacy feels like a messy debate that ultimately isn't great. While flagrantly defying social norms might be unWise (at the Enlightened level, if no other), it shouldn't be so because Wisdom is a measure of how much you bow to convention.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yeah, Hubris is the natural tendency of Awakened. Just like doing what vampires do naturally causes them to lose Humanity and doing what werewolves do naturally causes them to fall more on the Spirit end of the spectrum. If it was easy for a Mage to be Wise, it wouldn't make a very good Integrity stat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T06:58:03-08:00","id":1038399,"title":"","content":"Wasn't saying it should be easy to maintain; just that it shouldn't be based on an external locus but, rather, an internal one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T07:32:44-08:00","id":1038410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038399#post1038399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wasn't saying it should be easy to maintain; just that it shouldn't be based on an external locus but, rather, an internal one.</div> </div> </div> </div>Humility is a pretty internal locus. If you see yourself as greater than or above others, you have a hubristic mind. If you act on that sense of superiority, particularly at the expense of others, it is an Act of Hubris. Social Orrhodoxy, as it relates to Wisdom, is about knowing your place and acting accordingly. Violating taboos is an Act of Hubris not because the strictures of Wisdom are determined by an external source, but because doing so comes from a place of seeing yourself as above the social contract that the taboo transgresses.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T07:52:20-08:00","id":1038412,"title":"","content":"Now, see, where that runs into a hard problem for me is, just as an example, the Boston sample setting for Mage. In its history, the founders of the Stone Assembly were a sexist bunch, believing women were inferior and shouldn't be a part of the magical society; telling Awakened women (and usually poor men and non-whites) that they shouldn't practice magic because they just weren't capable of it like white male gentry were. This was not only the social orthodoxy of the Consilium (and Orders) of the time, but of Sleeper society as well. Indeed, for the latter, any sort of magic was outright devilry by their opinion.<br /> <br /> If we take the idea that social orthodoxy should determine Wisdom, <i>every</i> act of magic would be a Wisdom violation for these people; every spell cast or attempt to rise above their &quot;station&quot; in life would see their souls slowly come unraveled until they became one of the Mad.<br /> <br /> Now, far be it from me to say, incidentally, that the idea of a group of Bostonian proto-feminist witches committing great upheaval and turning the social order upside down in a great explosion of societal liberation is <i>not</i> utterly fantastic and something I'd pay to see, but it probably isn't where the Wisdom system was designed to run from.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T08:08:24-08:00","id":1038422,"title":"","content":"Almost every act of magic is a Wisdom violation if your Wisdom is high enough.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T08:21:00-08:00","id":1038427,"title":"","content":"So, a few pages later and no one has answered my question, near as I can tell. The only example of &quot;morality&quot; that keeps coming up is pre-meditated murder. Which has been admited to be strange and off, since a mobster hitman has been quoted as a possible canidate for High Wisdom despite all the premediated murders. <br /> <br /> Lets just ignore the pre-meditaed death and murder bits for a bit. What ELSE is there that makes Wisdom into &quot;Mage Morality.&quot; The only other thing I can possibly see argued is the Virtue/Vice bits. Seriously, I have no idea why they should have any effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T08:36:01-08:00","id":1038434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038412#post1038412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, see, where that runs into a hard problem for me is, just as an example, the Boston sample setting for Mage. In its history, the founders of the Stone Assembly were a sexist bunch, believing women were inferior and shouldn't be a part of the magical society; telling Awakened women (and usually poor men and non-whites) that they shouldn't practice magic because they just weren't capable of it like white male gentry were. This was not only the social orthodoxy of the Consilium (and Orders) of the time, but of Sleeper society as well. Indeed, for the latter, any sort of magic was outright devilry by their opinion.<br /> <br /> If we take the idea that social orthodoxy should determine Wisdom, <i>every</i> act of magic would be a Wisdom violation for these people; every spell cast or attempt to rise above their &amp;quot;station&amp;quot; in life would see their souls slowly come unraveled until they became one of the Mad.<br /> <br /> Now, far be it from me to say, incidentally, that the idea of a group of Bostonian proto-feminist witches committing great upheaval and turning the social order upside down in a great explosion of societal liberation is <i>not</i> utterly fantastic and something I'd pay to see, but it probably isn't where the Wisdom system was designed to run from.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then the question becomes what Tier it's an Act of Hubris against. If your transgression of the social order doesn't harm anyone, it should probably go at Enlightened Tier, alongside &quot;doing anything with magic that you could have safely done without magic.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T10:20:02-08:00","id":1038469,"title":"","content":"I think that's flimsy. After all, most strict societies consider violations of the social order as a form of harm unto themselves, and that an individual who harbours deranged ideas (like equality and personal liberty) does themselves harm as well.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038427#post1038427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, a few pages later and no one has answered my question, near as I can tell. The only example of &amp;quot;morality&amp;quot; that keeps coming up is pre-meditated murder. Which has been admited to be strange and off, since a mobster hitman has been quoted as a possible canidate for High Wisdom despite all the premediated murders. <br /> <br /> Lets just ignore the pre-meditaed death and murder bits for a bit. What ELSE is there that makes Wisdom into &amp;quot;Mage Morality.&amp;quot; The only other thing I can possibly see argued is the Virtue/Vice bits. Seriously, I have no idea why they should have any effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>The description of acts that violate Falling Wisdom as &quot;selfishly destructive&quot;, so there's that.<br /> <br /> Also, murder isn't the only act of violence on that list. The text also mentions violence in general (causing long-term in general), as does forcing someone to act counter to their interests.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T10:31:43-08:00","id":1038477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038469#post1038469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's flimsy. After all, most strict societies consider violations of the social order as a form of harm unto themselves, and that an individual who harbours deranged ideas (like equality and personal liberty) does themselves harm as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>That doesn't make those societies <i>right</i>. And anyway, Hubris doesn't really care about self-destruction. It cares about elevating yourself above others, especially by harming or degrading them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T11:39:54-08:00","id":1038500,"title":"","content":"Isn't self mutilation pretty much equal to self destruction, and self mutilation is considered to be Understanding Wisdom Sin?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T11:44:19-08:00","id":1038503,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038469#post1038469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, murder isn't the only act of violence on that list. The text also mentions violence in general (causing long-term in general),</div> </div> </div> </div> In the same breath as pre-meditated murder; the two are linked. I thought we were putting this aside?<br /> <br /> So, you are considering a list of things that includes destroying souls and making deals with the Abyss are considered morals, as is altering any being through magic. Alright, I can see where you're coming from. Thank you for answering.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 01-26-2017, 02:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T11:46:22-08:00","id":1038505,"title":"","content":"What's weird is the fact that it specifically mentions Awakened Souls, so you could read is as not caring about muggle souls, which is...interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T11:49:20-08:00","id":1038507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038505#post1038505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's weird is the fact that it specifically mentions Awakened Souls, so you could read is as not caring about muggle souls, which is...interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div> Considering that its related to making deals with the Abyss and destroying Supernal critters, I think that we're talking more on &quot;destroying magic&quot; as the degeneration point at Low Wisdom instead of anything else. Well, that and crimes of passion that rob you of your reason.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T12:45:44-08:00","id":1038528,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2102_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2102\" data-vbnamecard=\"2102\">MCN</a> - You're welcome.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038477#post1038477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That doesn't make those societies <i>right</i>. And anyway, Hubris doesn't really care about self-destruction. It cares about elevating yourself above others, especially by harming or degrading them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, as WHW points out, soulstones are a specific example of Falling Wisdom and self-destruction going hand in hand.<br /> <br /> As for those societies... Why doesn't it? Why buck social orthodoxy now when it was one of your listed reasons before? Why are we talking about harm now? Harm, even is contentious, as people disagree extensively about what harm means. We've had arguments in the real world lately over the psychic harm induced by being made to bake a cake (or to not be allowed to buy a specific kind of cake from a specific baker). <br /> <br /> Wisdom needs a set of ethics to refer to outside of general terms. Tried to do that with personalized lists but... Clunky.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T12:54:06-08:00","id":1038532,"title":"","content":"There's a difference between giving yourself extra work and a system being clunky.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T14:26:39-08:00","id":1038578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038528#post1038528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, as WHW points out, soulstones are a specific example of Falling Wisdom and self-destruction going hand in hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but arguably because it's a taboo use of magic, not because it's harmful to the Mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038528#post1038528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for those societies... Why doesn't it? Why buck social orthodoxy now when it was one of your listed reasons before?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because breaking with societal orthodoxy isn't an Act of Hubris because the society says it is. It's an Act of Hubris because breaking the orthodoxy requires elevating yourself above that society. The law itself isn't what Wisdom cares about, it's the pride in deciding that you are above the law, whatever the law may be. Now, if breaking the law is not otherwise Hubristic, it's a lower Tier Act of Hubris than violating a law that would be an Act of Hubris <i>per se</i> even if it weren't against the law. Just like an Act of Hubris is a lower Tier when it's performed by magic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038528#post1038528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why are we talking about harm now? Harm, even is contentious, as people disagree extensively about what harm means. We've had arguments in the real world lately over the psychic harm induced by being made to bake a cake (or to not be allowed to buy a specific kind of cake from a specific baker).</div> </div> </div> </div>Because several Acts of Hubris are on the list seemingly only due to being harmful. And it makes sense if you think about it, to harm another is to put yourself above them, a Hubristic act.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T17:23:40-08:00","id":1038654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038532#post1038532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a difference between giving yourself extra work and a system being clunky.</div> </div> </div> </div>Anything that slows down the game by requiring extra management without directly adding to it in proportion is clunky.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038578#post1038578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, but arguably because it's a taboo use of magic, not because it's harmful to the Mage.</div> </div> </div> </div>From the core:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Self-mutilating events such as soul stone creation risk degeneration. </div> </div> </div>It is specifically the self-mutilating aspect of it; soul stone creation is an <i>example</i> of self-mutilation, not a specific taboo.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038578#post1038578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because breaking with societal orthodoxy isn't an Act of Hubris because the society says it is. It's an Act of Hubris because breaking the orthodoxy requires elevating yourself above that society. The law itself isn't what Wisdom cares about, it's the pride in deciding that you are above the law, whatever the law may be. Now, if breaking the law is not otherwise Hubristic, it's a lower Tier Act of Hubris than violating a law that would be an Act of Hubris <i>per se</i> even if it weren't against the law. Just like an Act of Hubris is a lower Tier when it's performed by magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>In what way can you differentiate the two here? How can you break any law without declaring, tacitly or blatantly, that you know better? Every time you break a law, even just jaywalking, you are saying that the general rule shouldn't apply to you in this situation. You might be right! If the road is closed and no vehicles are allowed through, why walk all the extra distance to the crossing if where you want to go is right across the street? But ultimately, the rule doesn't care. It says you shouldn't do it, you did it anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1038578#post1038578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because several Acts of Hubris are on the list seemingly only due to being harmful. And it makes sense if you think about it, to harm another is to put yourself above them, a Hubristic act.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but what constitutes harm varies from person to person. Harm is a matter of perspective, not a feature of external reality. This is outright a feature of theocratic debate, wherein mainstream Christian dogma holds that eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil brought suffering and pain to Adam and Eve and their descendants, and was a source of ultimate harm, whereas the Gnostics, some modern Satanists, and, to an extent, Jewish mystics see the eating of the fruit and the expulsion from the Garden as liberating; a transition towards some higher state of being. What one group sees as the greatest mistake humanity ever made and a cruel pain inflicted on all of humanity by their own progenitors, another sees as liberation from ignorance and the right choice, even if suffering was the price of the ticket. These groups often even frame it as a question of hubris; humanity and Satan as hubristic and trying to usurp God's rightful place, or God as hubristic for trying to keep humanity from equaling it.<br /> <br /> It is a matter of perspective, and that's not a good thing to have in a system like this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-27T00:01:17-08:00","id":1038785,"title":"","content":"If Wisdom is internal, then it really is a matter of perspective, isn't it? Hence all those flexible Acts of Hubris, each Orders' bias towards certain forms of Hubris, and inurings. <br /> <br /> If it was external, based on some arbitrary set of ethics, then of course it wouldn't be a matter of perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-27T12:48:48-08:00","id":1039057,"title":"","content":"I'm not against the idea of some flexibility depending on the Mage's background, but IMO there should still be firmer consistent guidelines.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm still not sure why being Hubristic should make you less able to control Paradox.<br /> <br /> I understand why being more reckless might make you more unable to deal with Paradox (ie. killing in anger), but what does Paradox have to do with Hubris? Why does the Abyss creep in more when dealing with a Husbrisit Mage?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-27T12:55:03-08:00","id":1039061,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1039057#post1039057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not against the idea of some flexibility depending on the Mage's background, but IMO there should still be firmer consistent guidelines.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm still not sure why being Hubristic should make you less able to control Paradox.<br /> <br /> I understand why being more reckless might make you more unable to deal with Paradox (ie. killing in anger), but what does Paradox have to do with Hubris? Why does the Abyss creep in more when dealing with a Husbrisit Mage?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is kind of a &quot;why do D&amp;D Wizards have a limited number of spells they can cast per day?&quot; kind of question. That's just the way Magic works in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-27T14:09:01-08:00","id":1039094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1039057#post1039057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand why being more reckless might make you more unable to deal with Paradox (ie. killing in anger), but what does Paradox have to do with Hubris? Why does the Abyss creep in more when dealing with a Husbrisit Mage?</div> </div> </div> </div>A Mage who over Reaches does so because he believes that either he can handle it or he has to. This introduces a level of complexity to his magic that he can't always control and when a Paradox succeeds it opens a door to the Abyss that allows it to taint the spell. A Mage risks Wisdom when he deals with a successful Paradox, or the Abyss in general. In other words the lower a Mage's Wisdom the more likely it is that he's gotten comfortable with a certain amount of Abyssal risk in his haste to cast more powerful spells than he can actually control. Casting spells that you can't actually guarantee control of while being confident that you can get away with it unscathed is textbook hubris. That's why.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T08:45:31-08:00","id":1040007,"title":"","content":"It still doesn't make any sense. Technically making someone eat popcorn that hates popcorn can make you less able to control paradoxes. that's pretty silly","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T09:25:25-08:00","id":1040034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040007#post1040007\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It still doesn't make any sense. Technically making someone eat popcorn that hates popcorn can make you less able to control paradoxes. that's pretty silly</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, what?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T09:32:30-08:00","id":1040043,"title":"","content":"I think he's referring to the &quot;forcing a sapient being to act against its best interests&quot; line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T09:38:02-08:00","id":1040047,"title":"","content":"Oh. Well in that case, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> yes, when you use your magic to do something silly but stupid it has silly and stupid results.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T09:56:29-08:00","id":1040061,"title":"","content":"It's almost like the game treats using magic to deprive people of their own free will as serious business no matter how much you want to treat it as a joke.<br /> I'd consider that a feature, personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T10:55:42-08:00","id":1040092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1039057#post1039057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand why being more reckless might make you more unable to deal with Paradox (ie. killing in anger), but what does Paradox have to do with Hubris? Why does the Abyss creep in more when dealing with a Husbrisit Mage?</div> </div> </div> </div>The best I can do to explain it is this:<br /> <br /> Your mundane and magical actions are reflected in your Gnosis. That reflection is persistent until changed, and part of it can be seen as your Nimbus. In a way, your Gnosis has a life of it's own. That's why your Nimbus spreads along your Sympathetic connections - acting as a magnet for certain phenomena. The more you control yourself the more contained it is. The less you control yourself the further it spreads.<br /> <br /> If you do things without regard for consequences, your Gnosis will reflect that. Rash or ignorant actions always bring the risk of going against your interests or professed beliefs. If your Gnosis is saddled with contradictions, it has greater Sympathy to the Abyss - a Cosmic System Error. Paradox is an expression of the Abyss, so the more intense the contradictions within you, the greater the risk of getting infected with Paradox.<br /> <br /> The Abyss doesn't feed of evil. It feeds off stupid. Wisdom assumes that stupid = evil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T12:43:37-08:00","id":1040155,"title":"","content":"Inuring works because there is a sympathy in deciding to not give a shit about this particular metaphysical-structure-in-action's consequences with the manifestation of power unleashed.<br /> <br /> If you don't care what happens when you let this spell fly, why the fuck should the universe care? The instant Paradox is a reflection of your own lack of shit-giving about your lack of control for this particular action-of-consequence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T16:21:20-08:00","id":1040272,"title":"","content":"Sorry guys, I know you're trying to educate me, which I appreciate, and I'm not trying to start a flame war about this, but many of these Acts of Hubris have nothing to do with loss of control.<br /> <br /> I absolutely don't have a problem with reckless behavior and loss of control causing your Wisdom to drop and thus making it harder for you to control your magic. That just makes sense.<br /> <br /> Neverthess, how is it a loss of control to temporarily control a spirit into doing something for you, or using Forces to heat your coffee in the morning (which by the rules has ZERO chance of ever going off the rails).<br /> <br /> IMO it's the morality part that fails, where so many of you are trying really hard to make it all make sense when it really doesn't.<br /> <br /> Carefully planning someone's execution may be immoral, but how does it represent a loss of control? How does it invoke the Abyss? After all, the Abyss isn't evil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T16:26:33-08:00","id":1040278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040155#post1040155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you don't care what happens when you let this spell fly, why the fuck should the universe care? The instant Paradox is a reflection of your own lack of shit-giving about your lack of control for this particular action-of-consequence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also it encourages you to double up your inured spells with your Praxes and become a Mana tube.<br /> <br /> Addicts!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T16:47:57-08:00","id":1040290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1039057#post1039057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> but what does Paradox have to do with Hubris? Why does the Abyss creep in more when dealing with a Husbrisit Mage?</div> </div> </div> </div> Oh, oh! I know this one!<br /> <br /> When you cast a spell, you're pulling down threads from the Supernal and overwriting the Fallen World. In short, spells are actually the act of merging two realities (supernal and fallen). This action understandably creates friction. The fabric of reality stretches and, in some cases, rips. Those rips and stretches are the Paradoxes. The Abyss is the &quot;realm&quot; outside of reality that peeks in through one of those rips when paradox happens.<br /> <br /> Wisdom-as-control reflects how well you handle the merging of realities when you cast a spell, and how careful you finesse the merger so there's as little stretching or tearing as possible. Hubristic mages have less finesse, which causes more tears, which allows the Abyss to creep in more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 01-29-2017, 07:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T17:13:21-08:00","id":1040302,"title":"","content":"I like to think that Inuring means overwriting a Truth (&quot;Fireballing chickens is not Wise&quot;) by something blatantly Untrue (&quot;Fireballing chickens is totally Wise&quot;). Your singular Untruth pretending to be Truth creates a dissonance and clash that results in Paradox. Or something. I haven't sleep in 3 days and can't communicate too well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T18:08:45-08:00","id":1040331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040272#post1040272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Carefully planning someone's execution may be immoral, but how does it represent a loss of control?</div> </div> </div> </div>How did you get to the point of having to execute somebody in the first place?<br /> <br /> Let's get Doylist for a second: Murder is on the scale because Mage is a game set in a world where murder is a Big Deal \u2014 hence why it's advised to always be a breaking point for Integrity and a compromise for Cover, why werewolves and vampires who regularly kill humans tend to have weird folkloric quirks, why Begotten feed best on death and Created worry about murder by supernatural means in particular.<br /> <br /> Characters who routinely engage in murder maintain their metaphysical stealth and finesse through careful management of their magical identity. A mage who expects to kill people fairly often inures a spell she can use to kill with some reliability and possibly Dedicates a Tool or makes a soul stone symbolically compatible with her killing spell, just as a career spirit-binder inures himself to the fallout of binding spirits or a dedicated Reaper maintains their Wisdom while they do what dedicated Reapers do.<br /> <br /> The Awakened are still ostensibly human, and the parts of their psychology not tied up in spellcraft are still not numb to the consequences of killing people, especially when they have so many other options visible and available to them; the most common way around this, then, is to blunt the trauma by drowning it in the rush of magical workings. A spell is a discrete phenomenon that does not exist until the mage brings it into the world, and by denying responsibility for the acts of their creation, the mage lets a little more go wrong; if they don't intend to fully permit the Abyss to claim their children, they must be that much more careful in order to take advantage of the loophole they've forced into being.<br /> <br /> The Abyss contains/does-not-contain the Lords of All Undoing \u2014 magically-induced negation (of will, of self, of life, what have you) is a call of like to like, and the wizards wrap their charm-children in swaddling clothes of sanctum and sigil to keep the cold and the dark from spilling from their eyeless faces in response. The ends of things may be natural, but they are seldom unremarkable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T21:20:51-08:00","id":1040406,"title":"","content":"Sorry, it's very poetic, but I don't buy it. The Abyss isn't &quot;evil&quot;, so I don't see any reason why immoral acts should invoke it more readily.<br /> <br /> Personally I just think they wanted to make Wisdom strictly about controlling your magic and not violating things with Supernal resonance like souls, but they were worried that PCs would go on killing sprees if murder wasn't a Sin against Wisdom IMO.<br /> <br /> We can stop now. It's fine. We have some differences of opinion and that's ok. You can have the final word if you'd like. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I have some idea of how I'm going to run it in my game. I like the mechanics of containing Paradox with Wisdom and the mechanics of Inuring. I don't agree completely with the Acts of Hubris list, but I can modify it if I want. I'm not convinced the mechanics fit the fluff, but the mechanics are pretty good so I'll work out some modifications to the fluff to make it work.<br /> <br /> Thanks again everyone for having the debate.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1470,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","user":"Johnny Awesome","date":". 01-30-2017, 12:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T21:53:57-08:00","id":1040420,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040406#post1040406\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Abyss isn't &quot;evil&quot;, so I don't see any reason why immoral acts should invoke it more readily.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't know if someone said this already, but I'll just say this as a last word and in case you missed it through the wall of posts (because I know I did miss quite a few points, cause I didn't have enough mental energy to invest myself fully in a debate like this) :<br /> <br /> It's easy to think murder, mind-control, and all those other immoral acts are AoH because they're immoral or unethical in the eyes of some arbitrary universal code of conduct.<br /> <br /> They are not. They just happen to cross with mortal concepts of morality and ethics.<br /> <br /> They're considered Hubris because they mean that the mage has gotten so low that he's willing to do stuff that he knows that it will have irreversible repercussions with unforeseen side-effects, when he is, you know, a <i>wizard</i>. As in, someone who can legitimately call himself a Master of the Universe, given a few years of training.<br /> <br /> It's the equivalent of a politician (someone in a position of power, much like a mage) murdering his rival, when he could have either best him with a better campaign, better policy-making, better lobbying, or literally anything else.<br /> <br /> The world itself asks you, &quot;Did you <i>have </i>to do that? You have <b>magic</b>, for god's sake. Couldn't you have done better?&quot;<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T21:57:31-08:00","id":1040422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040420#post1040420\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Don't know if someone said this already, but I'll just say this as a last word and in case you missed it through the wall of posts (because I know I did miss quite a few points, cause I didn't have enough mental energy to invest myself fully in a debate like this) :<br /> <br /> It's easy to think murder, mind-control, and all those other immoral acts are AoH because they're immoral or unethical in the eyes of some arbitrary universal code of conduct.<br /> <br /> They are not. They just happen to cross with mortal concepts of morality and ethics.<br /> <br /> They're considered Hubris because they mean that the mage has gotten so low that he's willing to do stuff that he knows that it will have irreversible repercussions with unforeseen side-effects, when he is, you know, a <i>wizard</i>. As in, someone who can legitimately call himself a Master of the Universe, given a few years of training.<br /> <br /> It's the equivalent of a politician (someone in a position of power, much like a mage) murdering his rival, when he could have either best him with a better campaign, better policy-making, better lobbying, or literally anything else.<br /> <br /> The world itself asks you, &quot;Did you <i>have </i>to do that? You have <b>magic</b>, for god's sake. Couldn't you have done better?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The irreversibility of it as pertains a sustained pattern is definitely a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T03:42:46-08:00","id":1040537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040406#post1040406\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Abyss isn't &quot;evil&quot;, so I don't see any reason why immoral acts should invoke it more readily.</div> </div> </div> </div> Lets put aside Wisdom for a moment, and I kind of want to address this. I'm not trying to say anything about morals or not of Wisdom. But I think that this is kind of a misconception about the Abyss should be addressed.<br /> <br /> The Abyss isn't &quot;evil&quot; in the same way that Cthulhu isn't &quot;evil.&quot; In that its an ancient being beyond the ken of mortals, while evil is a paltry concept devised by mortals to reflect harmful things to themselves and society. The Abyss/Cthulhu just is, has &quot;existed&quot; before reality and will &quot;exist&quot; after reality is swallowed up by entropy and the heat death of the universe. There is no Creator God to devise laws to rebel against that holds sway over this eternal phenomeon. The universe is large and uncaring, and doesn't care for human definitions of good and evil. <br /> <br /> That said, that's just Lovecraft's definition of evil. Or, perhaps, a Christian one. By many real world standards, however, the Abyss and all its inhabitants pretty much are evil. The Abyss is <i>hungry</i> and doesn't care who it hurts. It wants to feed on and replace entire swaths of reality. Dealing with the Abyss inevitably leads to your brain cracking. This isn't just a stillborn reality that you can pick fluffy bunnies that were never born out of. Its pure destruction and chaos that wants to return everything to chaos. Bringing anything Abyssal into reality undermines reality itself and leads to destruction and upsetting anything resembling natural balance.<br /> <br /> But even that doesn't address why immoral acts invoke it. For that, I'm going to start pulling information out of Intruders - Encounters with the Abyss and the Abyss chapter of Summoners. Its broken - not only are the things inside the Abyss twisted and harmful, but you invoke them by doing horrific things to people and places so they become broken as well. Seriously - repeatedly mind controlling someone allows abyssal mind spiders to take physical root inside their brain. You can summon an abyssal succubus that exists purely to indulge brutal sex crimes through acts of human depravity and cruelty; well, that's how you summon pretty much every Acamoth. When you break something, even metaphorically, it allows the Abyss to seep in. Broken promises, meaningless deaths, destroying the very concept of space, and more are all used to call down the abyss. Moral acts tend to not break things, while immoral acts tend to break things.<br /> <br /> There's a very good reason why mages call the Supernal and the Abyss with names that are synonymous with Heaven and Hell. To mages, the Abyss pretty much is evil incarnate. You commit attrocities to pull it closer. The Abyss represents the closest thing to demon summoners in Mage (and not just goetic thought projections), and is fully presented with all the warnings and badness we expect out of real world demons, including their mere presence being dangerous to life itself. So, yes.<br /> <br /> In conclusion, the Abyss is evil, and immoral acts allow it greater purchase in the world than it would have otherwise. Its always been that way since the very beginning of 1e, and has not changed in 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 01-30-2017, 06:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T03:52:55-08:00","id":1040538,"title":"","content":"(delete and ignore please!)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 01-30-2017, 06:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T08:08:50-08:00","id":1040670,"title":"","content":"I don't buy that.<br /> <br /> Lightning and the vacuum of space aren't any more evil, but are just as careless and destructive. Abyssal spiders aren't Cthulhu, they just get summoned when a mage abuses magic and live out their natural life cycle. It's detrimental to our life, but so is most disease, so are termites, so is most of the natural world, and so are we to the natural world. If you're going to say that makes it evil, then everything's evil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T17:16:16-08:00","id":1040981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040670#post1040670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't buy that.<br /> <br /> Lightning and the vacuum of space aren't any more evil, but are just as careless and destructive. Abyssal spiders aren't Cthulhu, they just get summoned when a mage abuses magic and live out their natural life cycle. It's detrimental to our life, but so is most disease, so are termites, so is most of the natural world, and so are we to the natural world. If you're going to say that makes it evil, then everything's evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>The difference between the Abyss and outer space is that the Abyss <i>hungers</i>. It <i>wants</i>. Space just is, with no consciousness to speak of. Space doesn't <i>want</i> to force its detrimental properties of living things, because it doesn't have the ability to want in the first place. The Abyss and the things in it have at least some measure of intelligence, regardless of how alien. It <i>wants</i> into this universe, it <i>hungers</i> for the things in it. To at least some degree it seems to <i>know</i> what it's doing, and that is what makes it evil (depending on your definition of evil).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T17:27:55-08:00","id":1040989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040981#post1040981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difference between the Abyss and outer space is that the Abyss <i>hungers</i>. It <i>wants</i>. Space just is, with no consciousness to speak of. Space doesn't <i>want</i> to force its detrimental properties of living things, because it doesn't have the ability to want in the first place. The Abyss and the things in it have at least some measure of intelligence, regardless of how alien. It <i>wants</i> into this universe, it <i>hungers</i> for the things in it. To at least some degree it seems to <i>know</i> what it's doing, and that is what makes it evil (depending on your definition of evil).</div> </div> </div> </div>All things in the abyss do not have intelligence, and whether the Abyss itself is intelligent is up for debate elsewhere. The sapient things in the abyss want, yes, but things like the abyssal spiders just are. And a vacuum hungers as well, not consciously, but it sucks everything into it through pressure.<br /> <br /> And even if you did ignore space and those abyssal entities that can think and process, you're dealing with things that are willing to hurt others to survive, just like every animal, plant, and the vast majority of humankind.<br /> <br /> It is no more or less evil than the things in our natural world, and the simple fact that it's infinite suggests there would actually be benevolent entities of some sort within it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T17:47:14-08:00","id":1040995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040989#post1040989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> All things in the abyss do not have intelligence, and whether the Abyss itself is intelligent is up for debate elsewhere. The sapient things in the abyss want, yes, but things like the abyssal spiders just are. And a vacuum hungers as well, not consciously, but it sucks everything into it through pressure.<br /> <br /> And even if you did ignore space and those abyssal entities that can think and process, you're dealing with things that are willing to hurt others to survive, just like every animal, plant, and the vast majority of humankind.<br /> <br /> It is no more or less evil than the things in our natural world, and the simple fact that it's infinite suggests there would actually be benevolent entities of some sort within it.</div> </div> </div> </div>In the end, what is and isn't evil is determined by humans, and is usually used to describe things that they find dangerous and disagreeable. Unthinking forces aren't usually labeled as evil because evil is often attributed to things that can think (sometimes without regard the degree of intelligence behind those thoughts). People often have the habit of grouping like with like. So even though some Abyssal creatures have the intelligence levels of an animal, that might still be enough given that what they do and what they try to do negatively affects humanity, and then you have the ones that are intelligent which are much more easily classified by most as being evil. Now take into consideration humanities love of grouping things together, all of the Abyss can be considered evil.<br /> <br /> It's generally directly and explicitly harmful to humans in one form or another, promotes actions and ways of thinking that are already dubbed as being evil in most places, and combine it with the outright hostility of most of it's intelligent denizens and you have why the Abyss is evil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T18:06:36-08:00","id":1041008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040995#post1040995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the end, what is and isn't evil is determined by humans, and is usually used to describe things that they find dangerous and disagreeable. Unthinking forces aren't usually labeled as evil because evil is often attributed to things that can think (sometimes without regard the degree of intelligence behind those thoughts). People often have the habit of grouping like with like. So even though some Abyssal creatures have the intelligence levels of an animal, that might still be enough given that what they do and what they try to do negatively affects humanity, and then you have the ones that are intelligent which are much more easily classified by most as being evil. Now take into consideration humanities love of grouping things together, all of the Abyss can be considered evil.<br /> <br /> It's generally directly and explicitly harmful to humans in one form or another, promotes actions and ways of thinking that are already dubbed as being evil in most places, and combine it with the outright hostility of most of it's intelligent denizens and you have why the Abyss is evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on your own explanation you have why the Abyss is <i>called </i>evil by some, even likely a majority of modern mages, players to. It doesn't make it so, any more than vampires thinking blood tastes good or the Seers saying the Exarchs are super cool. When you're talking about a meta-mechanic like Wisdom, the in-character beliefs of the Abyss have nothing to do with it. Unless the Free Council are right, but that wasn't what was being supposed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-30-2017, 09:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T19:12:23-08:00","id":1041049,"title":"","content":"Abyss is not 'evil'. It's <i>Chaos</i>. Primordial Chaos. And like in D&amp;D or Warhammer - from this Chaos comes Order. Supernal ( in form of Arcana ) is all about ( secret ) Order to universe. There are hierarchies of power ( Gnosis ), there are levels of initiation (Arcana). Heck, even Supernal World could be said to work as 'putting in to boxes' most chaotic thing like 'magic based on all possible Symbols'. Like in <b>Left Hand Path</b> book chapter on Scelesti - For Fallen World to exist in semi-constant manner ( or exist in any meaningful way ) Order needed to win over Chaos. But just like <a href=\"https://1d4chan.org/wiki/Chaos_Gods_of_Law\" target=\"_blank\">Law gods of Warhammer universe are only (most powerful ) faction in all Chaos gods</a>, so can be said Supernal is most 'logical' or 'ordered' parts of Abyss.<br /> <br /> And Wisdom is this main principle of this universal Order in every mage. You can forsake it totally and become consumed by (chaotic) parts of Supernal magic - you will probably by more powerful than rest of normal Awakened - but you also be slave to it's Primordial Chaos parts ( if even if they still not be 'proper' Abyss you think about ).<br /> <br /> And this why Abyss in enemy to Wisdom - Wisdom is all about order and control of magic. Abyss is the most primal version of her - without limits and constrains. Defying <i>any </i>logic. And two forces fight with each other from the time when Supernal Symbols said no to the rest of Abyss 'it's boring to be ever changing - we will become stable and create our own pet project called Earth'. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-30-2017, 10:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T19:49:07-08:00","id":1041063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041008#post1041008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Based on your own explanation you have why the Abyss is <i>called </i>evil by some, even likely a majority of modern mages, players to. It doesn't make it so, any more than vampires thinking blood tastes good or the Seers saying the Exarchs are super cool. When you're talking about a meta-mechanic like Wisdom, the in-character beliefs of the Abyss have nothing to do with it. Unless the Free Council are right, but that wasn't what was being supposed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Evil is nothing but an artificial label created by humans anyways. Something is evil <i>because</i> people believe it is, not because of any sort of inherent supernatural quality. Humans have a reason to call the Abyss evil and proceed to do so, therefore it is evil (at least as far as humanity in general would be concerned). <br /> <br /> You grasped at it a bit earlier actually when you said that by this logic everything is evil. Everything <i>is</i> evil, and at the exact same time <i>nothing</i> is evil. It's all in the eye of the beholder, but the fact of the matter is, most humans would likely find the Abyss to be evil, and so it is. It is as long as people believe it is, because that's all it takes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 01-30-2017, 10:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T19:59:08-08:00","id":1041067,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041063#post1041063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Evil is nothing but an artificial label created by humans anyways. Something is evil <i>because</i> people believe it is, not because of any sort of inherent supernatural quality. Humans have a reason to call the Abyss evil and proceed to do so, therefore it is evil (at least as far as humanity in general would be concerned).<br /> <br /> You grasped at it a bit earlier actually when you said that by this logic everything is evil. Everything <i>is</i> evil, and at the exact same time <i>nothing</i> is evil. It's all in the eye of the beholder, but the fact of the matter is, most humans would likely find the Abyss to be evil, and so it is. It is as long as people believe it is, because that's all it takes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except we're talking about the mechanics of a game where the developers and writers can just say, 'The Abyss is evil.' and it is, as they have with other games. And saying it's evil because some people think it is, especially in an out of world discussion, is very misleading. The Abyss is not evil and working with it is not inherently evil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T20:05:36-08:00","id":1041071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041067#post1041067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except we're talking about the mechanics of a game where the developers and writers can just say, 'The Abyss is evil.' and it is, as they have with other games. And saying it's evil because some people think it is, especially in an out of world discussion, is very misleading. The Abyss is not evil and working with it is not inherently evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have they ever said that the Abyss or anything else was flat out metaphysically evil? I mean there's a lot of stuff that would be evil from a human perspective (the Abyss being one of them), but as far as I recall there's never been a stated objectively capital E evil force existing in the CofD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T20:15:22-08:00","id":1041074,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041071#post1041071\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have they ever said that the Abyss or anything else was flat out metaphysically evil?</div> </div> </div> </div>I was referring to other games, such as Dungeons and Dragons, where things are tagged with alignments. Coincidentally one of them is the Abyss.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean there's a lot of stuff that would be evil from a human perspective (the Abyss being one of them), but as far as I recall there's never been a stated objectively capital E evil force existing in the CofD. </div> </div> </div>Belial's Brood has the Adversary, who pops up again in the Inferno book. It's been a while but I believe Slashers are more or less attributed to evil, though there is an in-world theory that they are sort of a white cell in response to humanity's supernatural affliction.<br /> <br /> The Abyss, the developer has stated, is generally without moral. 'The Abyss doesn\u2019t have a morality (except when it does, as it is inherently contradictory) \u2014 it\u2019s a breakdown in reality. Abyssal intrusions poision the Fallen World around them as they interfere with the Fallen-Supernal duality. It can have beneficial effects, but they\u2019ll always come with a hefty side-serving of Zalgo.'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-30-2017, 11:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T20:27:35-08:00","id":1041082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041074#post1041074\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I was referring to other games, such as Dungeons and Dragons, where things are tagged with alignments. Coincidentally one of them is the Abyss.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Belial's Brood has the Adversary, who pops up again in the Inferno book. It's been a while but I believe Slashers are more or less attributed to evil, though there is an in-world theory that they are sort of a white cell in response to humanity's supernatural affliction.<br /> <br /> The Abyss, the developer has stated, is generally without moral. 'The Abyss doesn\u2019t have a morality (except when it does, as it is inherently contradictory) \u2014 it\u2019s a breakdown in reality. Abyssal intrusions poision the Fallen World around them as they interfere with the Fallen-Supernal duality. It can have beneficial effects, but they\u2019ll always come with a hefty side-serving of Zalgo.'</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh I know other games specify alignments (and the complaints run rampant about such things), but as far as I know CofD is more realistic in that it doesn't give things hard good/evil absolute color values. So while it the developers technically <i>could</i> have an pure good/evil thing going on, i'm fairly sure they specifically don't add that kinda thing in.<br /> <br /> Can't really comment on stuff from the Inferno, though I'm guessing its fairly old and almost certainly wouldn't be labeled pure evil if ever brought to 2e.<br /> <br /> And if the Abyss is without moral, then it specifically isn't metaphysically good <i>or</i> evil, though people can still do as we do and label it evil and it be as true as it is for anything else we label as such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T23:08:02-08:00","id":1041116,"title":"","content":"Personally, I view the Abyss and the Inferno in terms of John Carpenter movies. The Abyss is &quot;In the Mouth of Madness&quot; while the Inferno is &quot;Prince of Darkness&quot;. The creatures that direct Sutter Cane are horrifying. The horrors end up encouraging evil acts but evil is not their goal. In a more direct sense, the horrors instill madness. Horror isn't good, but it need not be evil. Everything associated with the Abyss seems to want to do bad things. Earthquakes, volcanoes, and tsunamis do bad things to the point that they are considered bad things. The Abyss is bad because it is a horrifying thing. If a mage goes around committing evil or maddening acts then they lower the defenses that make them human(-like). Do the wrong thing and you might let something bad in. I don't know how evil that may be, but it definitely ain't good, not good for anybody. I always saw a thin line between the Scelesti and the Mad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T04:48:36-08:00","id":1041195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't buy that.<br /> <br /> Lightning and the vacuum of space aren't any more evil, but are just as careless and destructive. Abyssal spiders aren't Cthulhu, they just get summoned when a mage abuses magic and live out their natural life cycle. It's detrimental to our life, but so is most disease, so are termites, so is most of the natural world, and so are we to the natural world. If you're going to say that makes it evil, then everything's evil.</div> </div> </div> </div> Lightning and the vacuum of space have nothing to do with the Abyss. Lightning is part of the Aether's symbols, and deep space part of Pandemonium's. The Abyss isn't an empty void. Its FILLED with aborted realities and things that should-not-be (at least by someone's standards). It has its own perverse ecology and chaotic energies separate from Creation's.<br /> <br /> You're missing the forest for the trees. The Abyssal spiders only <i>come into being through acts of hubris and abuse</i>. They're literally the manifestation of evil acts. And that is exactly what I'm explaining here. Disease and termites have their own balance with nature. Hells. Our bodies rely on certain microbes living in balance with us, or we die. Its an amazing, diverse system that even termites, despite a few destructive qualities, fit into and support*. The Abyss is something that comes from outside our balanced ecology with the sole purpose <i>of destroying it</i>. From destructive acts to create more destructive acts. The Abyss is never natural, never balances. Its introducing the magical equivalent to toxic waste into the environment and killing things, but worse. Its not even just trying to survive - it can do that just fine in the Abyss proper, and not try to eat our reality. But it does.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm not going to argue about the exact definition of evil here. That's impossible. But you can't compare the Abyss to natural phenomeon and say those are the same. They're not. That is not the purpose in the story, it does not fit the book's definition of the Abyss. That view is completely unsupported by the books. If you want to buy into Scelesti propaganda instead, go ahead. Your arguments are the same ones that the Scelesti use to justify their attrocities. But the Abyss isn't just something natural with some desctructive tendacies. It is irrevocably linked to anti-social and destructive actions, and propegate more of such actions. We see that again and again, not just in the descriptions of the Abyss, but in each and every abyss entity write up. If you want to say that's not evil? I'm not going to stop you. But the overwhelming majority of definitions people use for evil fits the Abyss. As well, it <i>doesn't matter</i> if the abyss is called evil or not. The point is that the abyss is irrevocably tied to the anti-social and destructive actions of people. These actions, by whatever name you want to call them, draw the Abyss closer into reality and allow it to manifest. And that's what's under discussion here. The link between the Abyss and &quot;morality.&quot;<br /> <br /> The only reason I've called the Abyss &quot;evil&quot; is to drive home a point. People who say that the Abyss is not evil are trying to treat it as some just impersonal force out there that can be beneficial or not just depending. These people are denying that there's a link between the Abyss and anti-social/destructive actions when, again and again, its driven home in the books that such actions are what call down the Abyss in the first place. <br /> <br /> Also? The Scelesti Ziggaurat for those of the Obrimos path is literally Cthulhu's temple. I'm not kidding - go read the description in Left Handed Paths. And that's not the only reference to Lovecraft's Great Old Ones whenever the Abyss comes up. So, mind spiders might not be Cthulhu itself, but something else in the Abyss absolutely is.<br /> <br /> *Seriously. Termites have a lot in common with worms in that they're a necessary part of soil engineering, necessary for plant growth and nutrition. Nature is so fucking awesome, it blows my mind whenever I learn something new about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All things in the abyss do not have intelligence, and whether the Abyss itself is intelligent is up for debate elsewhere. The sapient things in the abyss want, yes, but things like the abyssal spiders just are. And a vacuum hungers as well, not consciously, but it sucks everything into it through pressure. </div> </div> </div> Your view is unsupported by the books. Sorry, but this is your head cannon, not offical. The Abyss defies such easy categories such as &quot;intelligent&quot; or &quot;sapient.&quot; Its too chaotic for that. You're trying to give form to something that is, by definiton, uncategorizable. The Abyss can only be described as what they aren't.<br /> <br /> As well, when something is summoned from the Abyss into creation, its forced to take the form of something here, so it can be defined by that while its here, but its only a mask that the Abyss critter wears; its not the thing's true &quot;nature.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041049#post1041049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Abyss is not 'evil'. It's <i>Chaos</i>. Primordial Chaos. And like in D&amp;D or Warhammer - from this Chaos comes Order.</div> </div> </div> </div> To continue this line of thought... Were you all aware that in D&amp;D, all of creation may have sprang from the Chaotic Evil realm of the Abyss as well? And that doesn't stop the Abyss in that game from being tied to evil actions and destruction in order to drag creation back into the chaos of the bottomless Abyss?<br /> <br /> I mean, yeah, there's no &quot;Evil&quot; label in the CofD, but other than that, there's actually a wealth of things that the two have in common if you look beyond just the evil-for-evil-sake's cartoonish portrayal that sometimes shows up. Its amazing how much D&amp;D!Abyss and CofD!Abyss have in common. Hmm.... would that make the Exarchs the equivalent to the Archdevils? They're both divine embodiments of tyranny... I'll have to think more on that one later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T08:29:25-08:00","id":1041279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041195#post1041195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lightning and the vacuum of space have nothing to do with the Abyss. Lightning is part of the Aether's symbols, and deep space part of Pandemonium's. The Abyss isn't an empty void. Its FILLED with aborted realities and things that should-not-be (at least by someone's standards). It has its own perverse ecology and chaotic energies separate from Creation's.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it doesn't have its own order. It's an infinite sea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're missing the forest for the trees. The Abyssal spiders only come into being through acts of hubris and abuse. They're literally the manifestation of evil acts. And that is exactly what I'm explaining here. Disease and termites have their own balance with nature. Hells. Our bodies rely on certain microbes living in balance with us, or we die. Its an amazing, diverse system that even termites, despite a few destructive qualities, fit into and support*. The Abyss is something that comes from outside our balanced ecology with the sole purpose of destroying it. From destructive acts to create more destructive acts. The Abyss is never natural, never balances. Its introducing the magical equivalent to toxic waste into the environment and killing things, but worse. Its not even just trying to survive - it can do that just fine in the Abyss proper, and not try to eat our reality. But it does. </div> </div> </div>Except they can be summoned in, too, or come in through some sort of Abyssal verge. And the Abyss doesn't come with a purpose to destroy any more than a mosquito comes with a purpose to spread disease. It just happens to do that. You say the Abyss is never natural, but there's shards of the Abyss in every mortal, and primitive mages appreciated it as a natural part of the world.<br /> <br /> The Abyss is not what introduces this 'toxic waste' into the environment according to you, you've said mages do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, I'm not going to argue about the exact definition of evil here. That's impossible. But you can't compare the Abyss to natural phenomeon and say those are the same. They're not. That is not the purpose in the story, it does not fit the book's definition of the Abyss. That view is completely unsupported by the books. If you want to buy into Scelesti propaganda instead, go ahead. Your arguments are the same ones that the Scelesti use to justify their attrocities. But the Abyss isn't just something natural with some desctructive tendacies. It is irrevocably linked to anti-social and destructive actions, and propegate more of such actions. We see that again and again, not just in the descriptions of the Abyss, but in each and every abyss entity write up. If you want to say that's not evil? I'm not going to stop you. But the overwhelming majority of definitions people use for evil fits the Abyss. As well, it doesn't matter if the abyss is called evil or not. The point is that the abyss is irrevocably tied to the anti-social and destructive actions of people. These actions, by whatever name you want to call them, draw the Abyss closer into reality and allow it to manifest. And that's what's under discussion here. The link between the Abyss and &quot;morality.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Actually my arguments are from a meta standpoint, and scelesti tend to deal more with immediate problems with their arguments. If your argument is that the natural world has a natural balance, you're forgetting things like man-made disasters and actions like those that cause climate change and the introduction of invasive species. And from a more grand scale you're conveniently forgetting that there's a near-infinite sea of waiting disaster waiting just beyond our atmosphere. Space does not care about balance, the lack of dinosaurs could have attested for this if they weren't wiped out. As for the overwhelming majority of definitions used to fit the Abyss, from the developer himself you have it. It does not have a morality, it's a breakdown in reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only reason I've called the Abyss &quot;evil&quot; is to drive home a point. People who say that the Abyss is not evil are trying to treat it as some just impersonal force out there that can be beneficial or not just depending. These people are denying that there's a link between the Abyss and anti-social/destructive actions when, again and again, its driven home in the books that such actions are what call down the Abyss in the first place. </div> </div> </div>The developer said it can be beneficial, even though it can create weird effects. And before your labeling of the Abyss the thread was pointing out that Wisdom is not just about destructive actions, as you said so yourself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1040290#post1040290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When you cast a spell, you're pulling down threads from the Supernal and overwriting the Fallen World. In short, spells are actually the act of merging two realities (supernal and fallen). This action understandably creates friction. The fabric of reality stretches and, in some cases, rips. Those rips and stretches are the Paradoxes. The Abyss is the &quot;realm&quot; outside of reality that peeks in through one of those rips when paradox happens.<br /> <br /> Wisdom-as-control reflects how well you handle the merging of realities when you cast a spell, and how careful you finesse the merger so there's as little stretching or tearing as possible. Hubristic mages have less finesse, which causes more tears, which allows the Abyss to creep in more.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also? The Scelesti Ziggaurat for those of the Obrimos path is literally Cthulhu's temple. I'm not kidding - go read the description in Left Handed Paths. And that's not the only reference to Lovecraft's Great Old Ones whenever the Abyss comes up. So, mind spiders might not be Cthulhu itself, but something else in the Abyss absolutely is. </div> </div> </div>And the Seers are part of the world, too, I guess everything in the world is just like them? What kind of argument is that?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your view is unsupported by the books. Sorry, but this is your head cannon, not offical. The Abyss defies such easy categories such as &quot;intelligent&quot; or &quot;sapient.&quot; Its too chaotic for that. You're trying to give form to something that is, by definiton, uncategorizable. The Abyss can only be described as what they aren't. </div> </div> </div>You responded to the wrong person. Ashenrogue said it was intelligent and easily categorized.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Abyss and the things in it have at least some measure of intelligence, regardless of how alien. </div> </div> </div>You are trying to give form to something. You label it evil. I've been saying that that's not all. And that's an in-world deceit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/970583-abyssal-imago?p=971430#post971430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Scelesti reject the Supernal. Nasnasi Scelesti go as far as warping their Paths, so they summon Gulmoth instead of Supernal Entities and see different symbols when using their Sight. The Abyss contains multitudes, but it is fundamentally un-pinnable, un-nameable. It's slippery, and rejects definition.<br /> <br /> So the best thing the Diamond can do to combat the Scelesti is give them <i>names</i>, and invent a structure for them. Most Scelesti think being called a &quot;Baalim&quot; or whatever is a sucker's game, but those who do fall into the memetic trap become easier for the Diamond's hunters to find.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-31-2017, 11:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T12:01:37-08:00","id":1041381,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041195#post1041195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, yeah, there's no &quot;Evil&quot; label in the CofD, but other than that, there's actually a wealth of things that the two have in common if you look beyond just the evil-for-evil-sake's cartoonish portrayal that sometimes shows up. Its amazing how much D&amp;D!Abyss and CofD!Abyss have in common. Hmm.... would that make the Exarchs the equivalent to the Archdevils? They're both divine embodiments of tyranny... I'll have to think more on that one later.</div> </div> </div> </div>And Mount Celestia has multiple layers, where how enlightened you are determines on which layer you stand. Hmm... Where have I red about something like this before?<br /> <br /> Also, Abyss is probably more like D&amp;D Far Realms. Demons seem to be more like CofD Akathartoi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5973,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5973-prometheus","user":"Prometheus","date":". 01-31-2017, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5973-prometheus","name":"Prometheus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5973_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T12:48:57-08:00","id":1041418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041381#post1041381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Demons seem to be more like CofD Akathartoi.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those would be the Devils, given their approach (trying to corrupt through contracts and subtle influence).<br /> <br /> D&amp;D demons with their rampant and wanton destruction could definitely fit other Lower Depths realms","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Historian ~ <b><a href=\"http://www.nwodbrasil.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.cronistasdastrevasbr.com</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>I currently ST a... </b>MtAW 2e campaign called &quot;Axis Mundi - Si Vis Pacem&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/250-yuukale","name":"Yuukale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T16:02:23-08:00","id":1041542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1036789-wisdom-issues?p=1041381#post1041381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, Abyss is probably more like D&amp;D Far Realms. Demons seem to be more like CofD Akathartoi.</div> </div> </div> </div> The original D&amp;D Abyss included all the abomination overlords, including mindflayer and abolith gods. The far realm eventually co-opted that, but at the heart, there are still demon lords based on the same things as the Far Realm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T22:33:09-08:00","id":1041697,"title":"","content":"I think we hit something cool but totally not on topic of Wisdom. Don't worry, I will create new thread one in minute or two. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-06T18:22:38-08:00","id":1044801,"title":"","content":"I don't know if this thread has resolved the nature of Wisdom, but doesn't it work as intended?<br /> <br /> I know it has more or less come down to an argument of moral cause and effect, but doesn't the fact that it smells like imposing a value system kind of make sense?<br /> <br /> It could be argued that the mage continues to believe that her actions were appropriate, as I believe the Mad will attest; however, one could say that there is sympathy between AoH and the Abyss. The act is not decidedly good or bad, but for the purposes of the Abyss as described, it resonates therein.<br /> <br /> A mage deleting an SUV full of people for no other reason than just because might peak the theoretical intrigue of the Abyss (From over the shoulder it remarks, &quot;I agree/I liked that.&quot;) Not to argue that the Abyss is any measure of sentient, but that the ST can direct the attention of the Abyss to actions you take on a case-by-case basis. Degradation occurs when you fail and even if you (and your mage) disagree, the Abyss has established some sympathy with your character. (It is worth it to note that I'm also not using sympathy in the Space arcana sense. I merely wish to convey relation or synchronicity, but perhaps it is all the same.)<br /> <br /> A player can also argue their actions aren't reckless enough to warrant Abyssal sympathy, then bonuses could be given or the roll could be waived.<br /> <br /> The fact that Wisdom works the way it does seems indicative of the trappings of the Fallen World. A disagreeable circumstance of being human and both interpreting and being interpreted by forces far beyond your control. The Exarchs presumably reside beyond the reach of the Abyss and can dabble in whatever they damn well please without judgment. They are the strange Old Ones, that don't see themselves as evil, but unlike you they don't have to deal with the incessant shittery of Paradox trying to befriend them.<br /> <br /> I think it's even cooler to translate the idea of &quot;I'm not fucking crazy!&quot; directly into the character and to live that Madness. To thoroughly feel your character is right in his conviction and to be twisted by Paradox into a shape that other mages lament. The Mad are alien because their ideas are perceived as far removed, not because their ideas are decidedly evil. The consequence of perceiving an alien idea (or set of ideas) that could potentially harm you is the judgment that it is perceptively unpredictable and perhaps dangerous and/or evil (at the very least bad/negative/unwanted to some such person or article.)<br /> <br /> Cthulhu is, as I've interpreted, a being that by virtue of existing is understood as foreign and evil. This is more or less, as someone else noted, how I view the Abyss and why I believe attributing Wisdom loss to sympathy with the Abyss (or loss of control) is appropriate.<br /> <br /> The whole Inuring thing could be understood as coming to terms with that sympathy, or obscuring it from the Abyss, perhaps even a sort of contract with the Abyss. (&quot;We agree on this much, but nothing else!&quot;)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T02:59:23-08:00","id":1044972,"title":"","content":"I don't think Wisdom has any direct interaction with the Abyss.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}